const HCA_PATHS={"search":"<circle cx=\"10.5\" cy=\"10.5\" r=\"7.5\"/><path d=\"m16 16 6 6\"/>","home":"<path d=\"m3 10 9-7 9 7v10a1 1 0 0 1-1 1h-5v-7H9v7H4a1 1 0 0 1-1-1z\"/>","book":"<path d=\"M12 5v16M3 4h4a5 5 0 0 1 5 2 5 5 0 0 1 5-2h4v15h-4a7 7 0 0 0-5 2 7 7 0 0 0-5-2H3z\"/>","practice":"<rect x=\"3\" y=\"3\" width=\"18\" height=\"18\" rx=\"2\"/><path d=\"m9 7 7 5-7 5z\"/>","tools":"<path d=\"M4 7h16v13H4zM8 7V4h8v3M4 12h16M10 12v3h4v-3\"/>","growth":"<path d=\"M4 3v17h17M7 14l5-5 4 3 5-7\"/>","save":"<path d=\"M6 3h12v18l-6-4-6 4z\"/>","arrow":"<path d=\"M4 12h16m-6-6 6 6-6 6\"/>","shield":"<path d=\"m12 3 8 3v6c0 5-8 10-8 10S4 17 4 12V6zM8 12l3 3 5-6\"/>","room":"<path d=\"m3 7 9-4 9 4v11l-9 4-9-4zM3 7l9 5 9-5M12 12v10\"/>","clock":"<circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"9\"/><path d=\"M12 7v5l3 2\"/>","dialogue":"<path d=\"M3 4h18v13H9l-6 4zM7 8h10M7 12h7\"/>","team":"<circle cx=\"9\" cy=\"7\" r=\"3\"/><path d=\"M3 20v-3a6 6 0 0 1 12 0v3M16 4a3 3 0 0 1 0 6M18 14a5 5 0 0 1 3 5v1\"/>","spark":"<path d=\"m12 3 2.5 6.5L21 12l-6.5 2.5L12 21l-2.5-6.5L3 12l6.5-2.5z\"/>","download":"<path d=\"M12 3v12m-5-5 5 5 5-5M4 16v5h16v-5\"/>","check":"<path d=\"m5 12 4 4 10-11\"/>","preview":"<path d=\"M2 12s3.5-7 10-7 10 7 10 7-3.5 7-10 7S2 12 2 12z\"/><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"3\"/>","purpose":"<circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"5\"/><path d=\"M12 9v6M9 12h6\"/>","calendar":"<rect x=\"3\" y=\"5\" width=\"18\" height=\"16\" rx=\"2\"/><path d=\"M7 3v4M17 3v4M3 10h18M7 14h3M14 14h3M7 17h3\"/>","route":"<circle cx=\"5\" cy=\"5\" r=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"19\" cy=\"19\" r=\"2\"/><path d=\"M5 7v7a5 5 0 0 0 5 5h7M10 5h5a4 4 0 0 1 0 8h-4m2-3-3 3 3 3\"/>","research":"<path d=\"m9 3 5 2-4 9-5-2zM12 6l2 1M4 21h16M7 14a6 6 0 0 0 12-1M17 12l2-1M10 18v3\"/>","reflect":"<path d=\"M4 4h16v13H9l-5 4zM8 8h8M8 12h5\"/>","play":"<path d=\"m8 4 13 8-13 8z\"/>","pause":"<path d=\"M7 4h3v16H7zM15 4h3v16h-3z\"/>"};
const HCA_BANK={"communication":[{"id":"communication-core","question":"Priya, a ward nurse, has just explained a new discharge plan to Mr Okafor. His taxi is due in ten minutes and he has nodded throughout without asking anything. What most directly checks how clearly she explained the next step?","answers":["Ask, “So I know I’ve explained it well, how will you describe the plan to your daughter tonight?”","Summarise the three main points again, slowly, and ask whether he has any questions before the taxi arrives.","Give him the printed discharge summary, point out the key section and ask him to ring the ward if anything is unclear."],"correct":0,"rationales":["Asking him to put the plan in his own words puts the check on your explanation, not on him. Any gap shows while he is still on the ward and you can fix it.","A slower repeat feels thorough, but “any questions?” invites a polite no. Nodding is not evidence of understanding, so try a teach-back prompt instead.","Written information helps later, but it cannot show what he has understood now. Use the leaflet to support a teach-back check rather than to replace one."]},{"id":"communication-practice-2","question":"Ms Rahman has just been told by the practice nurse that her blood test needs repeating. As she picks up her coat she says, “I still don’t know what happens next.” The next patient is already waiting. What is the most useful response?","answers":["Go back over the whole conversation from the start in case she missed a detail, even if the clinic runs later.","Agree the next step aloud: who will book the test, by when, and what she should do if she hears nothing.","Point to the leaflet on the desk and invite her to phone reception once she has read it at home."],"correct":1,"rationales":["Repeating everything can bury the one thing she is missing. Find the unclear step first, then restate only that and check again.","This closes the loop. A clear next action, a named owner, a time frame and a safety-net tell her exactly what to expect.","A leaflet supports a conversation, but it leaves her question unanswered and hands the follow-up to her. Agree the plan before she leaves the room."]},{"id":"communication-practice-3","question":"A pharmacist has shown a man how to use a new inhaler device and wants to check he can manage it at home. Which wording keeps the check about her explanation rather than about him?","answers":["“You’ve got the idea, haven’t you? Just tell me if there is anything you are unsure about.”","“Can you repeat back what I just told you, so I know you were listening properly?”","“I want to be sure I’ve explained this clearly. Could you show me how you’ll use it at home?”"],"correct":2,"rationales":["A closed question that expects a yes makes it hard to admit confusion. Ask him to show or tell you instead.","This frames the check as a test of his attention, which can make people defensive or silent. Put the focus on how clearly you explained it.","Naming your own explanation as the thing being checked keeps the tone kind and invites an honest answer. Watching him demonstrate shows more than a verbal yes."]}],"safety":[{"id":"safety-core","question":"A man arrives on the ward from a care home. The transfer sheet lists a blood-thinning tablet that his daughter says was stopped last week, and the GP summary does not mention it. The drug round starts in ten minutes. What best addresses the uncertainty?","answers":["Go with the transfer sheet, since it is the most formal document, and note the daughter’s comment in his file.","Ask him which tablets he took this morning and follow his account, as it is the most recent information.","Hold that item and ask the pharmacist or prescriber to reconcile the three sources before it is given."],"correct":2,"rationales":["A formal document can still be out of date. Recording the daughter’s comment without acting on it leaves the discrepancy unresolved.","His account matters, but memory and confusion on transfer can both mislead. It is one source to compare, not the tie-breaker.","Reconciliation resolves a conflict between records and the patient’s account; it does not pick a winner by guesswork. Pausing the one uncertain item protects him while the answer is found."]},{"id":"safety-practice-2","question":"On an evening round, a nurse finds a prescription whose handwriting could be read as two different strengths. The prescriber has gone off shift and the next dose is due in an hour. What supports safer practice?","answers":["Give the strength that matches what the patient had yesterday, and leave a note for the morning team to confirm.","Pause and contact the on-call prescriber or pharmacist through the approved route to clarify before giving it.","Ask a colleague which strength she thinks it says and give it if you both read it the same way."],"correct":1,"rationales":["Using yesterday’s pattern is a sensible-sounding guess, but a guess it remains. A note for the morning comes after the dose has gone.","If an order is unclear, stopping to clarify is the safety action. An hour leaves time to ask, so the pause costs little.","Two people agreeing is not the same as the prescriber confirming. Shared misreading is a classic way an error passes a check."]},{"id":"safety-practice-3","question":"Dev, a healthcare assistant, nearly took the wrong patient to theatre until a colleague spotted that the wristband did not match. No one was harmed, and Dev feels embarrassed. How should the event be handled so the team can learn?","answers":["Report what happened, when, and what helped to catch it through the incident system, without assigning blame.","Mention it informally at handover and keep the formal report for when something actually goes wrong.","Write the report and name the colleague who handed over the patient, so the cause is clear."],"correct":0,"rationales":["A near-miss report shows where the system is fragile. Recording the facts and what caught the error lets the team fix the process before harm occurs.","An informal chat reaches only the people in the room and leaves no trace for the wider system. Near misses are the cheapest lessons you will get.","Naming a person tends to end the inquiry early and makes others less willing to report. Describe the steps and conditions instead."]}],"empathy":[{"id":"empathy-core","question":"Mrs Hughes, 78, was given a new daily exercise plan after her fall. At review she sighs and says, “It’s all a bit much, really.” You have ten minutes. What is the most useful response before you offer any solution?","answers":["“Let’s cut it down to the easiest exercises so it feels more manageable for you.”","“It sounds like a lot to manage. Which part is hardest at the moment?”","“I know it’s hard, but sticking with it is what will get you back on your feet.”"],"correct":1,"rationales":["Trimming the plan may be right in the end, but offering it first guesses at the problem. It might be the pain, the time or the stairs.","Naming the feeling and asking which part is hard shows you are curious rather than assuming. Her answer then tells you where to adapt the plan.","Explaining why the plan matters can sound like a reprimand. Understand what is getting in her way before making the case for the plan."]},{"id":"empathy-practice-2","question":"On a community mental health visit, Sam hesitates after you outline weekly group sessions and says, “Yeah, okay.” His shoulders drop and he glances at the door. What is the most useful next question?","answers":["“Most people find the group helpful once they are there. Shall I book you in?”","“Is the time a problem? I can note that you would prefer to start later.”","“You went quiet just then. What, if anything, worries you about the group sessions?”"],"correct":2,"rationales":["Reassuring him with what others experience closes the conversation before his concern is heard. Ask first, then reassure if it fits.","Guessing the barrier and offering a fix can miss the real issue, such as fear of being judged. Ask an open question to find out.","Noticing the hesitation and asking an open question lets Sam say what is really on his mind. A tentative yes is often not a real one."]},{"id":"empathy-practice-3","question":"A daughter tells you she is exhausted after a fortnight of hospital visits to her father. A call bell is sounding in the next bay and you have about a minute. Which response acknowledges her without overstepping your role?","answers":["“That sounds exhausting. What would help most over the next few days?”","“You are doing brilliantly, and he is in safe hands, so try not to worry too much.”","“I understand how that feels. I will make sure someone from the team rings you each day.”"],"correct":0,"rationales":["This validates the feeling and invites her priority, with no promises. It is also short enough to fit the minute you have.","Praise and reassurance mean well, but they can tell her that her feelings are not welcome. Acknowledge first, then ask what would help.","A promise outside your control sets her up for disappointment. Offer what you can actually do, and pass on the request through the right route."]}],"sbar":[{"id":"sbar-core","question":"At 02:00 you give an SBAR about a patient whose breathing has worsened. The on-call doctor says, “OK, noted, thanks,” and starts to hang up without saying whether she is coming. What best establishes a shared plan?","answers":["“Thanks. I will record in the notes that you have been informed about his breathing, with the time of this call.”","“Just to be sure it was clear: his breathing is faster than an hour ago and he is looking more tired.”","“Before you go, who will see him and by when, and who should I call if he worsens before then?”"],"correct":2,"rationales":["Documenting the call is useful but does not tell you who will act. A record that someone was informed is not a plan.","Repeating the findings checks that the message was heard, not that anyone will respond. The recommendation and ownership are what is still missing.","Ownership, timing and a contingency turn an acknowledged message into a shared plan. Asking before she hangs up is the last chance to close the loop."]},{"id":"sbar-practice-2","question":"A district nurse phones a GP about a patient with a hot, swollen leg. She has given the situation, background and her observations, and the GP asks, “So what are you asking me to do?” Which reply is the recommendation?","answers":["“I think it could be an infection, but I can’t be certain without a doctor looking at it.”","“I would like you to visit today, or tell me if I should arrange hospital review.”","“It has been swollen for two days and he was started on antibiotics last week.”"],"correct":1,"rationales":["This is your assessment of the problem, which is valuable, but it still leaves out what you want done and by when.","A recommendation states the action you need and when. It also gives the GP a clear choice to respond to.","That is background. It helps the GP picture the case but does not say what you are asking for."]},{"id":"sbar-practice-3","question":"You tell the night coordinator that a bed-bound patient’s family are asking for a senior review. She replies, “Yes, I’ve heard you,” and picks up another phone. Nobody has said who will go. What remains to do?","answers":["Ask directly who will speak to the family, by what time, and what you should do if that does not happen.","Take her acknowledgement as handing it over, and tell the family a senior colleague has been informed.","Send the same message to two more senior colleagues so at least one of them picks it up."],"correct":0,"rationales":["Being heard is not the same as taking ownership. A named person, a time and a fallback complete the handover.","Acknowledgement alone does not assign the task, and telling the family it is in hand may promise something no one has agreed to.","Widening the audience spreads responsibility thinner, so everyone can assume someone else is acting. Pin down one owner instead."]}],"infection":[{"id":"infection-core","question":"Between two patients on a bay, the shared blood-pressure cuff looks spotless. The first patient had no known infection and the next is waiting. What determines whether it is ready to reuse?","answers":["The cleaning or reprocessing step your local policy sets for that equipment between patients.","Whether the first patient was on additional precautions, since only then does the cuff need cleaning.","Whether anything visible is on it, because a clean-looking cuff has not touched anything harmful."],"correct":0,"rationales":["Standard precautions apply to everyone, so reuse follows the approved process for that item, not how it looks or who used it.","Waiting for an infection label misses the point of standard precautions. Equipment is prepared for reuse in the same way for every patient.","Contamination is often invisible. A visual check cannot replace the approved cleaning process."]},{"id":"infection-practice-2","question":"A nurse has just changed a dressing and is still wearing gloves when a call bell rings in the next bay. She says, “I’ll keep these on, they’re protecting me.” What fits standard precautions?","answers":["Keep them on for a quick task and clean hands at the end of the round instead.","Remove the gloves and clean hands at that moment, then put on a fresh pair for the next task.","Wipe the gloves with a sanitising wipe, which makes them safe to wear into the next bay."],"correct":1,"rationales":["Gloves can carry organisms from one patient to the next. Hand hygiene is needed at the moment of change, not later.","Gloves do not replace hand hygiene. Removing them and cleaning hands at the right moment protects the next patient and the nurse.","Gloves are not designed to be cleaned and reused between patients. The safe sequence is to remove them, clean hands and re-glove."]},{"id":"infection-practice-3","question":"A carer is giving oral care to a resident, wearing gloves and an apron. The resident suddenly vomits on the floor and the carer’s sleeve. What should guide the precautions she uses next?","answers":["Carry on with the same precautions, since she has already started and is wearing protective equipment.","Do what her colleague did at the last spill, as that worked and saves time on checking the policy.","Reassess what she may now be exposed to and follow local guidance for body-fluid spills and clothing."],"correct":2,"rationales":["Protection chosen for oral care may not cover a spill. Starting a task is not a reason to stop reassessing.","Copying habits passes on workarounds as well as good practice. Check against the local guidance for this exposure.","When the task changes, the likely exposure changes with it. Matching precautions to the new risk is what standard precautions mean in practice."]}],"reasoning":[{"id":"reasoning-core","question":"A man in the emergency department was labelled “anxiety, chest tightness” at triage. An hour later his observations have changed and he says the pain now spreads to his arm. What best supports a safe reassessment?","answers":["Take another full set of observations, and speak to the doctor only if they continue to worsen.","Tell the doctor what no longer fits the anxiety explanation and ask the team to consider what else it could be.","Ask the triage nurse whether she still thinks anxiety is the most likely explanation before going any further."],"correct":1,"rationales":["More observations are not wrong, but waiting to see a worse trend delays a concern you already have. Share the new information now.","Naming the mismatch out loud and opening up alternatives counters anchoring on the first label. It keeps the decision with the responsible team.","Asking the person who made the first judgement tends to bring back the same answer. Take the mismatch to the responsible clinician instead."]},{"id":"reasoning-practice-2","question":"A GP trainee is sure a child’s fever is a simple viral illness. The mother mentions the child has not passed urine since morning, which does not fit. What is useful?","answers":["Pause to ask what else could explain both, and explore the details that do not fit the viral picture.","Note the history as reported but keep to the viral plan, since viral fevers are very common at this time of year.","Ask more questions about typical viral symptoms to confirm the diagnosis before moving on."],"correct":0,"rationales":["A diagnostic pause asks what does not fit and what important alternative should not be missed. A single odd detail may be the most informative one.","Common things are common, but a common label should not replace the details in front of you. Record it and act on it.","Looking only for confirming information is a trap. Look for what would change the picture, not just reinforce it."]},{"id":"reasoning-practice-3","question":"A healthcare assistant has noticed a resident is more drowsy than at breakfast and goes to tell the nurse in charge. Which wording keeps what she saw apart from what she thinks it means?","answers":["“She’s had a poor night, so she’s just tired, but I thought I should mention it anyway.”","“She’s obviously coming down with an infection. Can you get a doctor to look at her?”","“She’s sleepier than at breakfast and hard to rouse. I’m worried it may be more than tiredness.”"],"correct":2,"rationales":["This slips an explanation in as if it were a fact and quietly plays down what she saw. Give the observation and let the nurse assess.","Stating a diagnosis as fact hides the actual observation and may anchor the nurse on it. Share what you saw and say you are concerned.","Observation first, then interpretation clearly labelled as a concern. This helps the nurse judge both what happened and what to do."]}],"speakup":[{"id":"speakup-core","question":"During a busy handover on a surgical ward, a newly qualified nurse interrupts to say, “I’m worried Mr Lee’s wound dressing is wetter than the chart suggests.” The consultant round starts in five minutes. What best supports speaking up?","answers":["Thank her, ask what she has seen, and have someone check the patient now before the handover carries on.","Ask her to jot the concern down so it can be discussed properly once the handover is over.","Explain the reasoning behind the current plan, then ask whether that changes her view."],"correct":0,"rationales":["Receiving a concern well is half of speaking up. Thanking her and checking straight away shows concerns are welcome and keeps the patient first.","Deferring may feel orderly, but it tells her that a safety concern can wait for a convenient moment. Quick checks cost minutes; missed ones cost far more.","Justifying the plan first can sound like “you are wrong”. Listen to what she observed before you defend anything."]},{"id":"speakup-practice-2","question":"A healthcare assistant has told the nurse that a confused patient’s bed alarm is not working. Two hours later it is still not fixed and he has tried to get up twice. How can she speak up so the concern is heard?","answers":["Mention it again, but soften it so the nurse does not feel criticised for the delay.","Use CUS: “I’m concerned, I’m uncomfortable, this is a safety issue,” and say what fix she needs.","Let it go, since the nurse has already been told once and will know how busy the ward is."],"correct":1,"rationales":["Softening can leave the message so gentle that it is missed a second time. A concern can be polite and still clear.","CUS gives a clear, patient-focused structure that is hard to mistake for a passing remark. Adding the specific action you need keeps it practical.","Telling someone once does not close a risk. If it is not acknowledged, repeat it clearly and escalate by the local route."]},{"id":"speakup-practice-3","question":"A student paramedic says to the crew leader, “I think we should double-check the patient’s allergy band before we go.” The crew leader is mid-handover and under pressure to move. Which response supports the student?","answers":["“We’ve done this a hundred times, but go on, have a look if it puts your mind at rest.”","“Next time, say it with more confidence, and tell me earlier rather than at the last minute.”","“Good spot, thanks. Let’s check it together now, then agree what we do next.”"],"correct":2,"rationales":["Granting permission grudgingly signals that checks are a favour, not a team habit. Treat the concern as legitimate and act on it.","Feedback on delivery can wait. Right now it reads as criticism of speaking up, which makes the next concern less likely.","Thanking, checking at once and agreeing the next step are what receiving a concern well looks like. It also encourages the next question."]}],"shared":[{"id":"shared-core","question":"Mr Banks, 71, has two reasonable options for his painful knee: twelve weeks of physiotherapy or a referral for surgical opinion. He lives alone and is his wife’s main carer. What should shape the next conversation?","answers":["Which option most patients of his age and circumstances have chosen, as that is the best guide to what works.","Which option is simpler for the team to arrange and explain, so the plan runs smoothly.","How each option’s benefits, risks and uncertainty fit with his caring role and what matters to him."],"correct":2,"rationales":["What others chose says little about what he values. Popularity is not the same as fit.","Convenience for the service can quietly steer the choice. The options should be compared on his terms, with the real trade-offs.","Shared decisions bring the evidence and the person’s priorities into one conversation. His caring role may change which trade-off matters most."]},{"id":"shared-practice-2","question":"You are explaining two reasonable ways to manage Mr Evans’s leg ulcer: weekly clinic visits, or a home dressing routine taught to a family member. He lives 40 minutes from the clinic. What should the conversation explore?","answers":["Which option the evidence slightly favours, so he is steered towards the stronger result.","What matters to him, the pros, cons and uncertainty of each option, and the practical barriers he faces.","Which option he is most likely to agree to, so the consultation can end on a clear decision."],"correct":1,"rationales":["Evidence belongs in the conversation, but steering him towards it skips his preferences. Present the options even-handedly, including uncertainty.","This covers the person’s priorities, balanced information and real-world obstacles, which are the core of a shared decision.","Aiming for a quick yes can feel efficient but leaves hidden worries unspoken. A decision that fits him will last longer."]},{"id":"shared-practice-3","question":"At an outpatient clinic, Ms Dar is offered two treatment options and says, “I’d like to go away and think about it.” The clinic is running forty minutes late. What is a useful response?","answers":["Ask what information or support would help her decide, and agree when and how you will follow up.","Explain that deciding today would avoid delay, and offer to book the first option provisionally.","Give her the leaflet on both options and tell her to contact the clinic when she is ready."],"correct":0,"rationales":["Wanting time is a normal part of deciding. Supporting it with the right information and a follow-up date keeps the decision open and shared.","A provisional booking can feel like pressure, even when kindly meant. She asked for time, so protect it.","The leaflet is helpful, but with no follow-up arranged she carries the whole burden. Agree who will contact whom, and when."]}],"ai":[{"id":"ai-core","question":"A colleague pastes an AI-generated summary into the team chat before your medicines safety meeting. It says a recent trial shows a benefit and cites “Hartley et al., 2023”, which nobody recognises. What is the strongest verification step?","answers":["Ask the tool for the full reference and the relevant quote, and accept it if it can supply both.","Find the original paper through a library or database and check it says what the summary claims.","Check that the journal looks credible and the year fits, then share it with a note that it needs review."],"correct":1,"rationales":["The same tool can generate a convincing reference and quote. Asking it to vouch for itself is not independent checking.","Only the source itself can show whether the claim is supported. AI tools can produce convincing citations that do not exist or say something else.","A plausible journal and date are easy to fake and say nothing about the claim. Circulating it first lets an unchecked claim spread."]},{"id":"ai-practice-2","question":"A student asks an AI tool about handover wording and receives a neat reference list. Before adding it to her portfolio, she wonders what the polished citations tell her. What do they establish?","answers":["That the tool has drawn on real sources, so a quick skim of each abstract will be enough.","That the answer has been reviewed, because tools seldom format references this well unless they are right.","Only that references were produced; she still has to confirm each source exists and supports the claim."],"correct":2,"rationales":["Tools can invent sources that look real, so skimming abstracts may be skimming nothing. Start by confirming that each one exists.","Neat formatting is a presentation feature, not a quality check. Keeping qualified human review in the loop is still your job.","Formatting is not verification. Each source has to be found and read before it can support a statement."]},{"id":"ai-practice-3","question":"A nurse wants help turning her messy shift notes into a clearer handover. Her trust has not approved any AI tools, and she is tempted by a free public chatbot. What belongs in it?","answers":["A made-up example containing no real details, and only if your organisation’s policy allows it.","Her notes with the patient’s name and date of birth removed, because without those they are anonymous.","A few lines such as the ward and bed number, since that is hardly any information."],"correct":0,"rationales":["Unapproved tools are for fictional, non-confidential practice material at most. Real notes should stay in approved systems.","Dates, places and rare details can still identify someone, so removing a name is not anonymisation. Keep real notes out of unapproved tools.","Small details can add up to an identifiable picture, and the real issue is that the tool is not approved. Use approved channels for anything real."]}],"wellbeing":[{"id":"wellbeing-core","question":"Aisha, a healthcare assistant, tells you she has not slept properly for weeks and keeps losing track mid-task. The shift has started and the ward is short-staffed. What addresses both the immediate and the continuing concern?","answers":["Suggest a quiet break and say you will check in with how she feels at the end of the shift.","Share the trust wellbeing leaflet and ask her to pick one idea from it to try this week.","Offer help now, like a short break, and encourage her to raise the workload with her manager."],"correct":2,"rationales":["A break helps for now, but with nothing to follow up the longer-term picture is left alone. Pair the break with the conversation about workload.","Resources can help, but handing them over leaves the load on her. Individual habits do not replace safe staffing and rest.","Fatigue is a signal, not a flaw. Immediate support helps today, and the manager conversation addresses the conditions behind it."]},{"id":"wellbeing-practice-2","question":"Leila, a community nurse, has eleven visits booked today and rarely sits down before 3 pm. She wants a small wellbeing habit that she can actually keep. Which action is most realistic?","answers":["A 45-minute walk before every shift, tracked in an app so she can stay consistent.","Eating lunch away from her car on three days this week, adjusting as the visits allow.","Waiting for a quieter month, then starting a full routine of exercise, meal prep and good sleep."],"correct":1,"rationales":["This asks for a lot of time in a day with none to spare, and one missed day can feel like failure. Aim smaller.","A small step that fits the day and can bend with it is far likelier to last than a perfect routine.","Waiting for the perfect moment often means never starting. A small adaptable action now builds momentum."]},{"id":"wellbeing-practice-3","question":"A newly qualified midwife says her breaks keep being missed because the unit is short-staffed. A friend suggests breathing exercises. When workload makes a personal reset difficult, what else may be needed?","answers":["Appropriate support and a conversation with her manager or occupational health about the workload.","A stricter personal routine so she can recover faster in the little time she does have.","Waiting until the busy period ends, since staffing pressures usually ease by themselves."],"correct":0,"rationales":["A reset can help in the moment, but persistent strain needs attention to working conditions too. Support and a workload conversation address both.","This puts the whole job of recovery on her, when the problem is that breaks are being missed.","Pressure that has no end date does not always ease. Raising it early gives the team a chance to respond."]}],"equity":[{"id":"equity-core","question":"Mr Nowak agrees to a new monitoring plan through his adult son, who speaks for him at the surgery. Nobody has asked about language, reading or how he gets to appointments. What is the best next step?","answers":["Ask Mr Nowak how he likes to receive information and whether an interpreter would help.","Give him the standard leaflet and ask his son to go through it with him when they get home.","Check he is happy to continue in English, then book the follow-up appointment if he says yes."],"correct":0,"rationales":["Asking him directly puts him at the centre and uncovers communication and access needs that his agreement may be hiding.","This assumes the leaflet and his son will suffice. A family member can help, but is not a substitute for qualified support or for asking him.","Asking is a good start, but a polite yes may reflect reluctance to be a bother. Explore what support would help, including practical access."]},{"id":"equity-practice-2","question":"A district nurse had assumed a retired lorry driver would be “practical” about self-caring for a leg wound. She now notices the dressings are untouched. What helps most?","answers":["Explain the steps again more firmly, since he is likely to respond to straightforward instructions.","Ask him respectfully what is getting in the way and what support he would prefer.","Record that he is not engaging and ask the GP whether self-care is suitable for him."],"correct":1,"rationales":["This repeats the same assumption about him with more confidence. Explaining again will not help if the barrier lies elsewhere.","Asking replaces assumption with information. The barrier could be eyesight, confidence, cost or something else entirely.","Labelling him as non-engaging closes the question before he has been asked. Find out what is behind it first."]},{"id":"equity-practice-3","question":"Mrs Ade uses British Sign Language and arrives for a clinic appointment with her husband. No interpreter is booked and the appointment starts in ten minutes. Which approach supports a more accessible consultation?","answers":["Write the main points on paper and ask her to nod to show she has understood each one.","Ask her husband to interpret for the appointment, since he is used to her signing at home.","Ask how she prefers to communicate and arrange a BSL interpreter before the consultation begins."],"correct":2,"rationales":["Written English may not be her first language, and a nod does not show understanding. Ask what works for her.","A family member is not a qualified interpreter, and speaking through him takes the conversation away from her. Offer proper support.","Qualified interpretation and her own preference should guide the format. Asking first respects her and avoids guesswork."]}],"improvement":[{"id":"improvement-core","question":"Task owners are often not named at handover on your ward. The manager suggests adding a mandatory “owner” column to the handover sheet across the whole hospital next month. Which next step supports learning from a small test?","answers":["Roll the new column out across the service and ask staff for feedback after a month.","Try a limited change on one shift for two weeks, with a measure and a check for added time.","Repeat the handover refresher session and count how many staff attend."],"correct":1,"rationales":["A wide launch gives you opinions, but little evidence of whether it helped or caused problems. Test on a small scale first.","A small, time-limited test with a measure and a balancing check lets you learn what works before committing the whole service.","Counting attendance measures activity, not whether owners are being named. Choose a measure that shows the effect you want."]},{"id":"improvement-practice-2","question":"Your care-home team believes missed evening fluids are down to short staffing around 6 pm. The manager wants to request an extra member of staff straight away. What should happen next?","answers":["Look for evidence that tests it, such as when fluids are missed and what else is happening then.","Treat staffing as the cause and request the extra cover, since it fits what everyone says.","Redesign the whole evening routine at once, so that every possible cause is covered together."],"correct":0,"rationales":["An explanation is a hypothesis until you test it. A little data may confirm staffing as the cause, or point to something else.","General agreement can feel like proof, but it is not evidence. If the real cause is different, extra cover will not fix it.","Changing everything together makes it impossible to tell which change helped. A smaller, targeted test is more informative."]},{"id":"improvement-practice-3","question":"A practice nurse notices that patients often miss their follow-up appointments. She wants to test a reminder slip handed out at the end of each clinic. Which plan contains what a small improvement test needs?","answers":["A clear aim, a rollout date for all clinics, and an email telling staff to use the slip.","A clear aim, the slip itself, and a note of how many staff liked it after the first week.","A clear aim, a limited approved trial, a measure of missed follow-ups, and a check for extra work."],"correct":2,"rationales":["Announcing a rollout is implementation, not a test. There is no measure to show whether the slip helped.","Staff opinion is useful context but does not show whether follow-ups improved. Add a measure of the outcome and a check for added burden.","This has an aim, a small approved change, a measure that matches the aim, and a balancing check for unintended burden."]}],"health-literacy":[{"id":"health-literacy-core","question":"During a discharge talk at 16:30, a woman who has followed everything so far goes quiet when you say “PRN”, “U&Es” and “OPD follow-up”. She has her coat on and her lift is waiting outside. What helps most?","answers":["Swap the abbreviations for everyday words, focus on one next action, and ask her to say it back.","Slow down and say the abbreviations more clearly, so she can pick up the terms as you go along.","Carry on at the same pace, since she was following well and has not asked you to stop or explain."],"correct":0,"rationales":["Plain words, one clear action and a teach-back check make the explanation usable. Her sudden quiet may be the only sign she has lost the thread.","Slower jargon is still jargon. Replacing the terms with everyday words helps far more than repeating them.","Silence after unfamiliar terms is often confusion in disguise. Agreement or quiet is not evidence of understanding, and many people will not interrupt."]},{"id":"health-literacy-practice-2","question":"A GP surgery is choosing how to send test results to Mr Brennan, 79, who mentioned at his last visit that his eyesight is getting worse. The admin team suggests the usual letter because it is cheapest. What should guide the format?","answers":["Use the standard letter, because most patients his age expect one and it keeps the surgery’s costs down.","Ask him which format works for him, such as large print, a phone call or a text, and record it.","Send the letter and ask his daughter to read it to him, since he seems to be well supported at home."],"correct":1,"rationales":["Cost and age-based assumptions should not decide this. If he cannot read the letter, the information has not reached him.","His own communication needs and preferences should decide, not cost or assumptions. Recording the answer means nobody has to ask again.","Relying on a relative takes away his privacy and choice. Ask him how he would like to receive his results."]},{"id":"health-literacy-practice-3","question":"A community nurse explains a twice-daily dressing routine to Mr Alvi, and he repeats the steps back perfectly. Then he mentions that he works night shifts and will not be awake for the morning dressing. What should happen next?","answers":["Repeat the explanation once more, stressing why consistent dressing changes matter for healing.","Record that he understands the plan and that any missed dressings are down to his own motivation.","Explore the practical barriers with him and involve the community nursing team to adapt the plan."],"correct":2,"rationales":["He already understands. Explaining the importance again will not fix a routine that does not fit his working pattern.","Calling it a motivation issue puts the blame on him for a plan that never fitted his life. Ask what would make it possible.","A clear explanation is not the finish line. Exploring the real-world barrier lets the team adapt the plan so it can work for him."]}],"risk-evidence":[{"id":"risk-evidence-core","question":"A leaflet for a new screening test says it “halves the chance of a serious complication”. The study behind it shows the risk falling from 4 in 100 to 2 in 100 over five years. A patient asks what the actual reduction is. What do you tell her?","answers":["50 percentage points, because the risk has gone from 4 down to 2, which is half of what it was.","2% of the original 4%, which is a very small proportion of the risk and only a minor change.","2 percentage points, which means 2 fewer people in every 100 over the five years."],"correct":2,"rationales":["Fifty per cent is the relative reduction, not the absolute one. Mixing them up makes the benefit look far larger than it is.","Two per cent of four is about 0.08 per cent, which is not what happened. The risk fell from 4% to 2%, a drop of 2 percentage points.","The absolute reduction is the difference between the two risks: 4 minus 2. “Halves” describes the same change in relative terms."]},{"id":"risk-evidence-practice-2","question":"A ward report says falls were 6 per 100 patients last quarter. A neighbouring ward reports 9 per 100 patients over the past year, and a manager says the first ward is therefore safer. What is needed before interpreting the difference?","answers":["Acceptance of the lower figure, since the first ward has had a better recent record on falls.","A comparison over the same time period and for similar patients before any conclusion is drawn.","A clearer chart with the two rates side by side, so the managers can see the gap properly."],"correct":1,"rationales":["The lower number may simply come from a shorter period. A fair comparison has to be like for like.","Rates need matching denominators, populations and time frames to be comparable. A quarter and a full year are not like for like.","A tidier chart does not fix a mismatched time frame. Better presentation of an unfair comparison is still unfair."]},{"id":"risk-evidence-practice-3","question":"A hospital reports that falls dropped by a third in the six months after it introduced a hydration chart. There was no comparison ward, and a new falls-reporting form was launched in the same period. What is justified?","answers":["That falls fell after the chart, an association that the other changes or chance might also explain.","That the chart caused the drop, because the timing lines up and the trust recorded no other change.","That other hospitals can expect the same drop, because the figures come from real patients on a real ward."],"correct":0,"rationales":["A before-and-after result shows an association. With no comparison and a second change in play, other causes cannot be ruled out.","Timing alone does not prove cause. A new reporting form, seasonal patterns or staffing could produce the same pattern.","Real data from one setting does not forecast another. Population, context and how the chart was used all matter."]}],"human-factors":[{"id":"human-factors-core","question":"On a medical ward, the end-of-shift check that bed alarms are switched on keeps being missed. Three reminder emails and a team talk have not changed it, and the night shift is starting to feel blamed. What is a useful next step?","answers":["Send a firmer reminder and ask each nurse to confirm in writing that they have read it.","Watch the task as it happens, ask staff where it gets difficult, and test a cue at that step.","Look at who was on shift when it was missed and arrange extra training for those nurses."],"correct":1,"rationales":["A stronger reminder is still a reminder. A repeated omission points to a design problem, not a concentration problem.","When reminders fail, look at the work itself. Watching it and asking those who do it reveals a fragile step a support can fix.","Singling out individuals assumes the cause is personal. If several people miss the same step, the process is the likelier culprit."]},{"id":"human-factors-practice-2","question":"A theatre team trials a new pre-list checklist for two weeks. The lead wants to find out whether it causes problems as well as whether it helps, so she needs a balancing measure. Which one should she choose?","answers":["The number of lists on which the checklist was fully completed, compared with the first week.","The rate of near misses reported before and after the checklist was introduced.","The extra minutes the checklist adds to turnaround, and any checks it duplicates."],"correct":2,"rationales":["This is a process measure: it shows whether people are using the checklist, not what the checklist costs them.","This is an outcome measure and shows the intended benefit. A balancing measure looks instead for side effects.","A balancing measure looks for unintended burden. Added time and duplicated checks show what the change costs."]},{"id":"human-factors-practice-3","question":"A manager redesigns the discharge-lounge pathway from the written policy and plans to launch it on Monday. A porter says the sequence “won’t work on Monday mornings” because of when the pharmacy deliveries arrive. Why involve the people who do the work?","answers":["They can reveal the constraints and workarounds that the written procedure does not show.","Their agreement shows the redesign will work in practice, so a small trial is no longer needed.","Their involvement replaces formal approval, since they understand the work better than managers."],"correct":0,"rationales":["People doing the task know the interruptions, equipment and timing that a policy cannot capture, and that is how fragile steps get found.","Staff input improves the design but cannot replace testing it. A limited trial still shows what happens on a real Monday.","Involvement and approval do different jobs. Frontline insight is valuable, and the change still goes through the approved route."]}],"med-transitions":[{"id":"med-transitions-core","question":"A resident comes back to the care home after a hospital stay. A medicine for his blood pressure is on the discharge list but is not on the care home’s own record, and the senior carer asks you what that means. What can you conclude?","answers":["The hospital probably stopped it, so the care home record is right and the discharge list can be set aside.","The care home missed it, so it should be added now, since the hospital list is the more recent document.","There is a discrepancy that needs verifying with the pharmacist or prescriber before anything changes."],"correct":2,"rationales":["This guesses at the reason. A medicine missing from one record may have been left off by mistake.","Newer does not always mean correct, and adding a medicine by inference is a prescribing decision. Ask the responsible clinician.","A mismatch is a question to resolve, not an instruction. Verification comes before any start, stop or change to a medicine."]},{"id":"med-transitions-practice-2","question":"A pharmacy technician is reconciling a patient’s list on admission. The GP summary, the discharge letter and his dosette box all disagree, and his wife says he stopped one tablet because it made him dizzy. Whose account is relevant?","answers":["The most recently updated electronic entry, since it should reflect the latest decision made.","The patient and his wife, alongside the approved sources, with differences passed to the pharmacist.","The ward team’s expectation of what he needs, as they know the patient’s likely condition best."],"correct":1,"rationales":["The latest entry could contain an error or miss a change made elsewhere. It is one source among several.","The person taking the medicines is a key source. Combine their account with the records and have the differences resolved by the right person.","Expectations are not evidence of what he actually takes. Base the list on the records and the person’s own account."]},{"id":"med-transitions-practice-3","question":"The pharmacist rings the GP and confirms that a duplicate medicine was entered on a patient’s list in error and should be removed. You are covering the ward and the patient goes home tomorrow. What follows?","answers":["Update the authorised record through the approved process, and tell the receiving team and the patient.","Make a mental note and mention the outcome at handover, since the pharmacist has already confirmed it.","Cross the entry out on your own copy of the list, which stops it being repeated for now."],"correct":0,"rationales":["Resolving a discrepancy is only complete when the verified plan reaches the record, the team and the person it affects.","Memory and a verbal mention can fail at the next shift change. The authorised record is where the plan has to live.","A private copy corrects nothing for anyone else. Update the authorised record so everyone sees the same plan."]}],"deterioration":[{"id":"deterioration-core","question":"During a routine 20:00 handover on an older-people ward, you notice Mrs Pike’s breathing is faster and she seems newly confused. The coordinator is halfway through the SBAR for bed 12 and the oncoming team is waiting. What takes priority?","answers":["Interrupt and use the immediate local escalation pathway for Mrs Pike, then return to the handover.","Let the coordinator finish a clear SBAR for bed 12, so the handover is complete before you speak up.","Write the change on the handover sheet and make sure it is raised at the next routine review."],"correct":0,"rationales":["An urgent concern does not wait for a tidy script. Escalating first keeps the patient’s safety ahead of the handover process.","A complete handover matters, but a few minutes of delay can matter more to Mrs Pike. Communicate significant concern before certainty arrives.","A note is not a call for help. Writing it down and waiting leaves a change in condition without anyone acting on it."]},{"id":"deterioration-practice-2","question":"On a night shift, a nurse reads a morning note saying “Comfortable, observations stable.” Tonight her patient looks flushed and clammy and she is worried. A colleague says, “He was fine today.” What does the earlier note establish?","answers":["That he is likely to be fine overall, so tonight’s appearance is probably a one-off to keep an eye on.","What the situation was earlier; it does not cancel a change you are seeing in front of you now.","That reassessment can wait until the next scheduled observations, which are due in about two hours."],"correct":1,"rationales":["Assuming a one-off is a form of anchoring. A change from his baseline is exactly what should prompt a closer look.","An earlier reassuring note is context, not a verdict. Current observations and your own concern take priority.","Waiting for a scheduled round could cost time that matters. If something looks different, reassess and escalate now."]},{"id":"deterioration-practice-3","question":"Ten minutes ago the registrar said, “Okay, noted,” to your call about a patient who is getting more drowsy. No one has arrived and you have heard nothing more. What remains to do?","answers":["Give it a little longer, as the registrar may be dealing with an emergency and has acknowledged you.","Record that the registrar was informed at the time of your call, and continue with routine observations.","Call again by the escalation route, say the patient has not been seen, and ask who is coming and when."],"correct":2,"rationales":["Acknowledgement is not action. If the concern is still unresolved, re-escalate through the local route rather than guess why.","Documentation records the call but does not bring help. Re-escalate and keep the patient under close observation.","Keeping the loop open means re-escalating when no action follows. Asking for a named responder and a time confirms ownership."]}],"data-stewardship":[{"id":"data-stewardship-core","question":"A student nurse posts a learning example in a study group chat. It has no name, but says “a 34-year-old with a rare genetic condition at the small unit on Skye, admitted last Tuesday”. A classmate says it is anonymous. Is it?","answers":["Yes, because the name has been left out and a person’s age alone does not identify them.","Not necessarily, because rare details, place and dates together may point to one person.","Yes, provided it is only shared once and stays within a closed group of fellow students."],"correct":1,"rationales":["The risk lies in the combination of age, condition, place and date, not in any single item.","Combinations of details can identify someone even without a name. Removing the name is not the same as anonymising.","A single share or a closed group does not make information anonymous. The details are the issue, and so is the channel."]},{"id":"data-stewardship-practice-2","question":"A clerk emails a clinic letter to the wrong “J. Patel” on the autocomplete list. She spots the error within a minute and feels dreadful. The clinic is about to start and she is tempted to quietly sort it out herself. What helps?","answers":["Tell her manager and report it through the local incident process promptly, noting what was sent.","Recall the message, delete the sent copy, and treat it as dealt with if the recall appears to work.","Email the wrong recipient asking them to delete it, then wait and see whether they reply."],"correct":0,"rationales":["Prompt reporting lets the organisation contain the problem and protect the patient. Keep the facts and follow the local process.","A recall may fail or come too late, and the recipient may have read it already. The incident still needs reporting.","Asking and waiting leaves containment to chance and delays the proper report. Start the incident process promptly."]},{"id":"data-stewardship-practice-3","question":"A receptionist uses an AI tool to draft a letter to a patient about a missed appointment. It looks polished and professional, and she has two minutes before the post goes. What does the polished format establish?","answers":["That the content has been checked, as a tool would not usually produce well-formatted errors.","That the practice permits its use, because the tool opens easily on the practice network.","Presentation quality only, so accuracy and suitability still need checking before it is sent."],"correct":2,"rationales":["Tools can produce errors in a perfectly tidy format. How a draft looks says nothing about whether it is accurate.","Easy access is not approval. Check that the tool and the use are permitted under information-governance policy.","A fluent output can still be wrong or inappropriate. Independent checking is the step that makes it safe to use."]}],"teachback-skill":[{"id":"teachback-skill-core","question":"You have just explained to Mr Dean how to use his home sample kit for a bowel screening test. He looks tired, says “right, yes” and has asked nothing. The clinic is running behind. Which prompt invites teach-back?","answers":["“I want to check I’ve explained that clearly. How would you describe the next step?”","“Does all of that make sense to you, or would you like me to go through it again?”","“Can you tell me the main points back, so I know that you took everything in properly?”"],"correct":0,"rationales":["The prompt names your explanation as the thing being checked and asks him to say what happens next in his own words.","A yes-or-no question about understanding is easy to answer with a polite yes. Ask him to describe the step instead.","This puts the test on the listener. Asking him to prove he has listened can make people anxious and defensive."]},{"id":"teachback-skill-practice-2","question":"A man asked to describe how he will record his blood-pressure readings at home gets the timing wrong. His wife starts to correct him, and he looks embarrassed. What should happen next?","answers":["Tick the teach-back box since he got most of it right, and give him a leaflet to cover the rest.","Thank him, explain the unclear part a different way, and ask him to describe it again.","Go through the whole explanation again, using the same wording as before but at a slower pace."],"correct":1,"rationales":["Mostly right is not good enough when the wrong part is the important one. A leaflet does not close the gap.","Teach-back is a loop. Naming the gap kindly, re-explaining in a new way and checking again repairs it without blame.","The words that did not land the first time may not land now. Change how you explain it, then check again."]},{"id":"teachback-skill-practice-3","question":"A student nurse on placement says teach-back sounds like a quiz and worries that patients will feel tested. Her mentor asks her to say, in one sentence, what is actually being checked. Which answer is right?","answers":["Whether the patient has retained the information, so that it can be recorded as understood.","Whether the patient is ready to follow the plan safely once they are back home on their own.","How clear the explanation was, and whether the two of you now share the same understanding."],"correct":2,"rationales":["Retention is part of it, but recording the person as having understood makes it their pass or fail. Teach-back is about your explanation.","Readiness matters, but this treats the check as an assessment of the person. The aim is to see if your message has landed.","The check is on the clarity of the explanation. Framing it that way keeps it supportive and invites honest answers."]}],"conflict":[{"id":"conflict-core","question":"At the 07:30 handover, two nurses disagree about who is chasing a patient’s scan result. You raise it with Dan, who said yesterday that he would. Several colleagues are listening. Which opening keeps the disagreement actionable?","answers":["“You said you’d chase it yesterday and didn’t, so this keeps happening whenever it’s your turn.”","“I’m sure nobody means to leave things without an owner, so let’s not make a thing of it.”","“The scan result has no named owner on the sheet. Can we agree who is chasing it?”"],"correct":2,"rationales":["It links the problem to Dan’s character, which tends to prompt defence. Describe the missing owner instead.","Smoothing it over avoids the awkwardness but leaves the scan result with no owner. Name the gap kindly and ask for an agreement.","It describes what you can see, makes no judgement about motive, and invites a specific agreement."]},{"id":"conflict-practice-2","question":"You think a colleague skipped a bed-rail check on your shift. When you ask her about it, she says the patient asked for the rails to be left down and the nurse in charge agreed. What helps next?","answers":["Tell her that her account does not match what you saw, and ask her to follow the policy next time.","Ask what she saw and was told, and keep the focus on the shared aim of keeping him safe.","Assume she is covering herself and make a note of the exchange for the nurse in charge."],"correct":1,"rationales":["Opening with a correction treats your view as the only one. Hear her account before you reach a conclusion.","Exploring her account and the common goal opens up the whole picture. She may hold information that you do not.","Inferring motives closes the conversation and may be unfair. Ask what happened before you conclude anything."]},{"id":"conflict-practice-3","question":"You have twice told a senior colleague that she signs off double-checks without reading the labels. Both times she laughed it off, saying she has done this job for twenty years. The concern remains unresolved. What next?","answers":["Use the organisation’s escalation route, such as your manager or the Freedom to Speak Up Guardian.","Raise it a third time in front of the team, so that she cannot brush it off again.","Let it rest, because you have done your part and you need to keep working alongside her."],"correct":0,"rationales":["When a serious concern stays unresolved, the formal route is there to support you. Escalating is not disloyal.","A public challenge may feel decisive but can humiliate her and harden positions. Use the proper channel.","Protecting the relationship at the cost of safety leaves the risk in place. Keep the concern moving."]}],"documentation":[{"id":"documentation-core","question":"You pick up a patient’s notes at the start of a late shift. The last entry, written at 14:20 by a colleague who has gone home, reads: “Team aware. Follow up later.” You are not sure what, if anything, you should do. What is missing from the entry?","answers":["A fuller account of what was discussed with the team, and who was present at the time.","A named person who will act on it and a time by which it should happen.","A list of the staff who were told, so the team can see exactly who is already aware of it."],"correct":1,"rationales":["More detail about the discussion could help, but without an owner and timing the next person still does not know who must act.","A record that someone else can act on has an owner and a time. “Follow up later” could mean anyone, at any time.","Naming who knows is not the same as naming who acts. Awareness without ownership leaves the task unclaimed."]},{"id":"documentation-practice-2","question":"A care assistant is writing up the morning for a resident who has been off his food. She wants her entry to keep what she saw separate from what she thinks it means. Which sentence does that?","answers":["“Mr Hall was clearly unwell at 08:10 and refused his breakfast because of it.”","“Mr Hall seemed a bit off at 08:10 and was not his usual self at breakfast.”","“Mr Hall refused breakfast and looked pale at 08:10. I’m concerned he may be unwell.”"],"correct":2,"rationales":["This states a cause as if it were a fact. The next reader cannot tell what was actually seen.","Phrases like “seemed off” are vague and blend impression with fact. Say what you saw, such as “refused breakfast”.","It states the observation with a time, then labels the interpretation as a concern."]},{"id":"documentation-practice-3","question":"At 16:45, a community nurse finishes a visit and writes a detailed, timestamped note about a patient’s worsening breathlessness in the electronic record. She is due to finish at 17:00. Is writing it enough?","answers":["No; a record may not be read in time, so she should also phone it through by the direct route.","Yes, because the note is detailed and timestamped, and the GP team will see it when they log in.","Yes, as long as she asks the surgery receptionist to read the record when she has a spare moment."],"correct":0,"rationales":["Documentation does not replace urgent communication. A direct call puts the concern in front of someone who can act.","A good note helps whoever reads it, but nobody may read it tonight. An urgent concern needs a conversation.","This hands an urgent concern to someone who may not be clinically placed to judge it. Contact the right clinician."]}],"risk-dialogue":[{"id":"risk-dialogue-core","question":"A leaflet says a medicine “cuts the risk of an event by 20%”. In the study, the event happened in 10 per 100 people without it and 8 per 100 people with it, over the same year. A patient asks you to explain. Which explanation is balanced?","answers":["A 20% reduction, which is the standard and widely understood way of describing the benefit.","8 in 100 had the event, so the other 92 in 100 were protected by taking the medicine.","2 fewer in 100 had the event that year, set alongside the possible side effects and the uncertainty."],"correct":2,"rationales":["A relative figure on its own hides the baseline. “20%” could mean 2 in 100 or 20 in 100 depending on where you start.","Many of those 92 would not have had the event anyway. Counting them all as protected overstates the benefit.","It uses the same denominator and time frame, states the baseline and puts harms alongside benefits."]},{"id":"risk-dialogue-practice-2","question":"A practice nurse uses an icon array of 100 faces to explain a screening result to Mr Zhou, who is not confident reading English. She has the picture on screen and ten minutes left. What should accompany the visual display?","answers":["A reassurance that the picture shows the best choice for someone in his position.","A plain-language explanation, and a prompt to hear how he interprets the picture.","A printed copy of the picture for him to take home and study in his own time."],"correct":1,"rationales":["Balanced numbers do not choose for the person. Telling him what is best removes the choice they are meant to inform.","A visual can support, but not replace, an accessible explanation. Asking what he sees checks his reading of it.","A copy may help later, but without an explanation and a check he may take away a misunderstanding."]},{"id":"risk-dialogue-practice-3","question":"Mrs Kaur understands that a treatment lowers her risk by 2 in 100, but she says the daily hospital trips would cost her her job as a bus driver. The clinician is ready to book the first appointment. What should happen next?","answers":["Explore that priority with her and involve the responsible clinician to look at alternatives.","Restate the benefit as a percentage, since that is the stronger way of making the point.","Conclude gently that she has not grasped the numbers, and offer a simpler diagram."],"correct":0,"rationales":["Her priority is as relevant as the numbers. Exploring it together with the clinician keeps the decision shared.","Repeating a bigger-sounding figure pushes her towards a choice rather than exploring what matters to her.","She did understand the numbers. Assuming otherwise because she disagrees overlooks her priorities."]}],"feedback":[{"id":"feedback-core","question":"You supervise a student nurse who forgot to name an owner for a task at handover. You have ten minutes at the end of the shift, and she is tired and a little anxious. Which feedback is most actionable?","answers":["“At handover, nobody was named for the review. Let’s practise saying who will do it.”","“You need to be more careful with handovers, because the detail really matters in this job.”","“That was a decent effort overall, and with a bit more practice I’m sure you will get there.”"],"correct":0,"rationales":["It describes an observable behaviour and offers something specific to try next.","This is a general comment about care that gives no specific behaviour to change.","Kind and encouraging, but vague. The student cannot tell what to keep doing or what to change."]},{"id":"feedback-practice-2","question":"A preceptor wants to give feedback to a junior doctor who left a ward round without handing on a task. Before sharing her own view, she asks, “How did that round feel to you?” Why does she ask?","answers":["It lets the learner agree with her view, which makes the feedback easier for them to accept.","It can reveal context she had not seen and help shape a next step that fits.","It lets her avoid discussing the impact of the missed task, which could feel harsh."],"correct":1,"rationales":["The aim is not agreement. A good question is open and may lead somewhere you did not expect.","Asking first treats the learner as a partner and may uncover constraints she did not know about.","The impact should still be discussed. Asking first helps the conversation, but it does not replace it."]},{"id":"feedback-practice-3","question":"At the end of a placement debrief, a mentor and student have talked through three things that went well and one that did not. They are both looking at the clock. What completes a feedback conversation?","answers":["A written summary of strengths and weaknesses for the student’s portfolio.","An overall rating that tells the student where they currently stand on the placement.","A manageable action to try, the support needed and a time to review it."],"correct":2,"rationales":["A summary records the discussion but does not give the student something to try.","A score without a next step tells the student where they are but not how to move forward.","Feedback becomes useful when it leads to a next step. An action, support and a review point close the loop."]}],"debrief":[{"id":"debrief-core","question":"After a stressful resuscitation simulation, the team leader opens the debrief. The team is tired and a little tense, and two members are checking the time. Which question opens a useful debrief?","answers":["“What went wrong, and who needs to do something differently next time?”","“What did we expect to happen, and what actually happened as the scenario unfolded?”","“Did everyone feel it went well enough for us to move on to the next scenario?”"],"correct":1,"rationales":["It sounds constructive but assumes something went wrong and points towards individuals. Start with expectations and events.","Comparing plan and reality invites facts from every role without hunting for a culprit.","This invites a quick yes and closes down learning. A debrief needs room for what made things difficult."]},{"id":"debrief-practice-2","question":"After an interruption-heavy medicines round, the team agrees on three good ideas to reduce disruption. The meeting ends with, “Let’s all keep these in mind.” Nobody has been named to do anything. What is missing?","answers":["One agreed action with a named owner, plus a date on which the team will review it.","A written summary of the three ideas shared with the wider team by email.","More ideas from the quieter team members who have not yet spoken in the meeting."],"correct":0,"rationales":["A debrief needs follow-through. A single action, owner and review date turn discussion into change.","Sharing the ideas helps, but without an owner nothing is certain to happen.","Wider input is good practice, but adding ideas does not solve the missing owner and review point."]},{"id":"debrief-practice-3","question":"A ward holds a kind, thorough debrief after a patient fall. Afterwards the sister says, “That covers it, so there’s no need for an incident form.” Does the debrief replace formal reporting?","answers":["Yes, a well-attended debrief captures the learning, so a form would only duplicate it.","Yes, a supportive debrief looks after staff better than a formal report ever would.","No; the formal incident report is still needed alongside the learning discussion."],"correct":2,"rationales":["Good attendance does not meet the reporting requirement. The report reaches people the debrief cannot.","Support matters, but reporting is a separate duty. Both can be done kindly.","A debrief supports learning and a report supports the organisation’s wider oversight. They do different jobs."]}],"humility":[{"id":"humility-core","question":"You are the registrar presenting a patient on the ward round. Your first thought was a urine infection, but the blood results do not quite fit and the consultant is waiting for your plan. Which statement shows useful humility?","answers":["“My working view is a urine infection, but the bloods don’t fit, so let’s check what else it could be.”","“I’ve seen this many times and I’m confident, so let’s only revisit it if she gets any worse.”","“It could be a urine infection, though honestly I’m not really sure about anything on this case.”"],"correct":0,"rationales":["It separates a working explanation from a verified fact, names what does not fit and says what is needed next.","Experience is valuable, but treating a working view as settled invites anchoring. Leave room for what does not fit.","Broad uncertainty is hard for the team to use. Useful humility names what is unclear and what would resolve it."]},{"id":"humility-practice-2","question":"At the evening medicines round, Mr Singh tells the nurse, “That’s not the tablet I normally take. Mine are white and round.” The record shows no change to his medicines. What helps?","answers":["Explain that the record is correct and that this is the same medicine under a different brand.","Take it seriously, check with the pharmacist, and keep an open mind about the record.","Note his comment but go ahead, as nothing is recorded and he may simply be confusing his tablets."],"correct":1,"rationales":["That may be true, but it is an assumption until it has been checked. Verify first, then explain.","A patient noticing a mismatch is useful information. Checking it and staying open is safer than assuming the record is right.","Dismissing him because the paperwork says otherwise treats the record as more reliable than the person. Explore it."]},{"id":"humility-practice-3","question":"New results show that the team’s earlier explanation for a patient’s dizziness was wrong, and the plan has changed. The ward round has finished and his daughter is due to visit in an hour. What should follow?","answers":["Update the plan in the record quietly, so that the notes are correct for the next team.","Hold off telling anyone until the next ward round, so that it can be explained properly.","Tell the patient and relevant colleagues what has changed and what happens next."],"correct":2,"rationales":["The record matters, but updating it silently leaves the patient and colleagues unaware of the change.","Delay leaves people acting on the old plan. A short, timely message is better than a perfect explanation later.","Revising an explanation is only useful if the people affected know about it and the next steps."]}],"dignity":[{"id":"dignity-core","question":"Mrs Adeyemi, who has mild hearing loss, attends clinic with her daughter. The daughter answers every question you ask, and Mrs Adeyemi has stopped looking up. You have a full waiting room. What supports her dignity?","answers":["Let the daughter answer, as she is used to speaking for her mother and it will save time.","Ask the daughter to wait outside, so that the answers come from Mrs Adeyemi on her own.","Speak directly to Mrs Adeyemi, and ask her how she would like her daughter to be involved."],"correct":2,"rationales":["Habit is not consent. Letting the daughter lead may sideline Mrs Adeyemi in her own appointment.","Excluding the daughter without asking is another assumption. Ask Mrs Adeyemi what she prefers.","Addressing her directly and asking about involvement respects her as the person receiving care."]},{"id":"dignity-practice-2","question":"A care worker is helping an 86-year-old resident who has had a stroke and finds it hard to find words. She is tempted to use very simple phrases in a slow, loud voice. What should guide communication support?","answers":["Use short, simple phrases and speak slowly and clearly, since that suits most older residents.","Ask him and his family what helps, and use the communication aids the service provides.","Use the same approach as with other residents, as it keeps the routine consistent and fair."],"correct":1,"rationales":["Slow, simplified speech can feel patronising and may not help. Age is a poor guide to communication needs.","His own needs and the service’s approved support should shape how you communicate, not assumptions about age.","A consistent routine is useful, but a standard approach may not meet his needs. Ask what works for him."]},{"id":"dignity-practice-3","question":"A nurse says, “I’m just going to take your blood pressure,” and wraps the cuff round the arm of a patient who is looking at the ceiling. The patient has not spoken. What makes permission meaningful here?","answers":["Explain what you are doing and why, then pause so they can ask questions or decline.","Treat their silence as agreement, because they have not objected or pulled their arm away.","Check the name band and the room number, then start now that the right patient is confirmed."],"correct":0,"rationales":["Real permission needs the purpose explained and room to say no or ask a question.","Silence can mean fear, confusion or resignation. It is not consent.","Identification is essential for safety but is not consent. Permission needs a conversation with the person."]}],"just-culture":[{"id":"just-culture-core","question":"A newly qualified nurse gave a medicine a few hours late after a ward move and a missing drug chart. Her manager wants to understand what happened. What does a systems approach to the review require?","answers":["Setting aside what she personally did and looking only at the system, so that no one is blamed.","Understanding the conditions around the error, together with fair accountability for individual actions.","Beginning with the newest member of the team, since inexperience is a common reason for errors."],"correct":1,"rationales":["Ignoring individual actions is not fair to anyone. A fair process looks at conduct as well as context.","A just culture balances system factors and personal accountability. Neither alone gives the full picture.","Starting with the newest person is blame by default. Begin with the facts and the conditions."]},{"id":"just-culture-practice-2","question":"A care assistant noticed a bed rail was unlocked just before a resident was moved. Nothing happened, and a colleague shrugs and says, “No harm, no foul.” What is useful here?","answers":["Fix the rail and remind the team to check, since nothing actually went wrong this time.","Wait to see whether it happens again, which will show whether it is a real problem.","Report it through the approved process so the team can learn from it."],"correct":2,"rationales":["Fixing the rail is right, but without a report the wider team cannot see the pattern.","Waiting for a repeat risks the harm you hoped to prevent. Report it now.","A near miss is a free lesson. Reporting lets the system fix the cause before someone is harmed."]},{"id":"just-culture-practice-3","question":"After a missed handover item on a ward, the manager calls a team meeting and the whole team is nervous about who is to blame. Which response supports learning?","answers":["Gather the facts and context, then use the organisation’s fair process to decide next steps.","Share the nurse’s name and what she missed in the team group chat so that people learn from it.","Send a reminder email about the handover checklist and consider the matter closed."],"correct":0,"rationales":["Learning needs the facts, the conditions and a fair process. It avoids both blame and brushing it aside.","Naming her publicly is punitive and discourages reporting. Use the established process.","A reminder alone rarely changes a system problem and skips the investigation. Look at conditions too."]}],"accountability":[{"id":"accountability-core","question":"A senior colleague asks you, a support worker, to update a patient’s care plan after a risk assessment. Only a registered nurse can sign that off, and she has to rush to a call bell. What is the accountable response?","answers":["Do it carefully using your best judgement, as it needs doing and she has asked you.","Say you will have a look at it, then leave it unless she brings it up again later.","Say it is outside your role, ask who can do it, and make sure that person is told."],"correct":2,"rationales":["Willingness is admirable, but doing it beyond your authority puts the patient and you at risk.","A vague acceptance leaves a silent gap. Say clearly what you cannot do and who can.","Accountability includes recognising the limit and routing the task to the right person."]},{"id":"accountability-practice-2","question":"You promised a family a call-back by 3 pm about their father’s discharge, but at 2.40 pm you are tied up with an unwell patient. The call is going to be late. What helps?","answers":["Wait until the patient is settled, then ring the family and explain why you were late.","Tell the family or a colleague early, and agree who will make the call and by when.","Tick the call off the list, since the unwell patient comes first and the family will understand."],"correct":1,"rationales":["Late is better than never, but the family waits anxiously. Tell them as soon as you know.","Early communication and an agreed alternative stop the commitment becoming a silent gap.","Marking it done when it is not hides the gap from the team. Be honest about what is outstanding."]},{"id":"accountability-practice-3","question":"At the end of a late shift, a referral you started is half completed and the night team has just arrived. You are due to leave in ten minutes. What should you do?","answers":["State clearly at handover what is outstanding, and who is taking it on.","Leave a note in the patient’s record, so the next team will see it when they read the file.","Finish it in your own time after the shift, so that the next team does not inherit it."],"correct":0,"rationales":["Making the status and the receiving owner explicit keeps unfinished items visible at transition.","A note helps, but the next team may not read it in time. Say it aloud and confirm who owns it.","Staying on is generous but not sustainable or visible. Hand it over properly."]}],"compassion-boundaries":[{"id":"compassion-boundaries-core","question":"A distressed relative stops you in the corridor and asks you, a healthcare assistant, to promise that her mother will not die this week. You have no information about the prognosis. Which response is compassionate and realistic?","answers":["“I can see how frightened you are. I can’t promise that, but I can ask the nurse to talk to you.”","“I’m sure she will be fine. She is in the best place and we are looking after her very well.”","“Lots of families feel like this at first. It really helps if you can try to stay strong for her.”"],"correct":0,"rationales":["It acknowledges her fear, is honest about your limits and offers something you can really do.","Reassurance you cannot back up may feel kind now but can hurt later. Be honest about what you can say.","It plays down her feelings and puts pressure on her to cope. Acknowledge the fear instead."]},{"id":"compassion-boundaries-practice-2","question":"After a distressing death on the ward, the charge nurse offers the team a five-minute pause before the next drug round. A colleague asks what a short reset like this can do. What does it address?","answers":["The emotional impact of the event, so that further support is not usually needed.","A moment to steady yourselves; it does not resolve workload or lasting distress.","The staffing pressure behind a busy shift, because brief breaks reduce strain overall."],"correct":1,"rationales":["A pause can help, but for many people the impact lasts longer. Offer follow-up support as well.","A reset is useful for the moment. Persistent strain needs support and attention to workload.","Breaks help individuals but do not change staffing. Workload needs its own attention."]},{"id":"compassion-boundaries-practice-3","question":"A carer tells you she has started drinking more to cope with looking after her husband, who has dementia. She asks what you would do. This is beyond your role. What helps?","answers":["Offer to be available whenever she needs to talk, so that she does not feel she is coping alone.","Share some tips that helped you through a hard time of your own, since sharing builds trust.","Acknowledge how hard it is and help her reach qualified support, such as her GP or a carers’ service."],"correct":2,"rationales":["Promising open-ended availability sets a boundary you cannot hold. Point her to a service that can.","Your experience may comfort, but it is not support for something beyond your role. Connect her to qualified help.","Listening and connecting her to the right service is compassionate and stays within your role."]}],"inclusive-leadership":[{"id":"inclusive-leadership-core","question":"At a team meeting about changing visiting hours, a healthcare assistant who usually has plenty to say stays silent throughout. The manager is keen to finish on time and records a consensus. What can you infer?","answers":["They agree with the plan, otherwise they would have said so, as they usually do.","Their view is unknown, so create another way for them to contribute.","They have no strong view on the matter, so the meeting can go ahead as planned."],"correct":1,"rationales":["Assuming agreement from silence risks missing a concern. Ask in a way that is easy to answer.","Silence tells you nothing about agreement. Offering different routes for input brings out views that would be missed.","Quiet does not mean uninterested. Give them a chance to contribute before deciding."]},{"id":"inclusive-leadership-practice-2","question":"A 4 pm team meeting is regularly missed by night staff and part-time colleagues, who then hear about decisions second-hand. As the ward manager, which meeting change would best support inclusion?","answers":["Share the materials in advance and offer more than one way to contribute, such as a form or a short call.","Run a shorter meeting with a quick decision round, and carry on with whoever can attend.","Ask each person to give a view on the spot, so no one can hold back from the discussion."],"correct":0,"rationales":["Accessible materials and several routes to contribute let more people take part.","A shorter meeting does not help those who cannot be there. Add routes for them to take part.","Putting people on the spot can silence those who need time. Offer ways to prepare and contribute."]},{"id":"inclusive-leadership-practice-3","question":"You asked the team for comments on a new rota, received several, and have now made a decision. Some of the comments will not be reflected in it. What closes the loop?","answers":["Thank everyone and tell them their comments have been filed for the record.","Report that most people supported the plan, as the comments were mainly positive.","Explain the decision and show how people’s comments shaped it, even where you could not act."],"correct":2,"rationales":["Filing the comments records them but tells people nothing about their effect.","A majority summary hides minority concerns and does not show how comments shaped anything.","Showing how input was used is what makes the invitation matter, even where you could not act on it."]}]};
const HCA_FINALES={"handover":{"event":"In this fictional handover, a colleague replies: “I have heard the concern, but the review is still unassigned.”","question":"Which next message most clearly closes the remaining gap?","answers":["“Thank you. I will assume the review will happen.”","“Can we confirm the named owner, agreed timing and what to do if the plan is not carried out?”","“I will mention it again at the end of the week.”"],"correct":1,"rationales":["Hearing a concern does not establish responsibility.","The message makes ownership, timing and contingency explicit. Urgent concerns require the immediate local route.","A later discussion does not resolve the current responsibility."]},"teachback":{"event":"The fictional patient describes the plan differently from what you intended. They are worried about asking again.","question":"Which response best keeps the conversation useful?","answers":["“I have explained it already.”","“I will record that you agreed.”","“Thank you for explaining. I need to make that part clearer. Let us try it another way and check again.”"],"correct":2,"rationales":["Repetition with blame can discourage someone from describing a gap.","Agreement does not establish understanding.","Acknowledge the response, clarify the explanation and check understanding without blame."]},"ai-case":{"event":"You find the actual source. It studies a different population from the one described in the fictional AI output.","question":"How should that affect your use of the claim?","answers":["Do not generalize the claim; examine the actual population, outcome and uncertainty with appropriate review.","Use the claim because the reference is real.","Ask the AI to sound more certain."],"correct":0,"rationales":["Finding a real source is only one step. Relevance and the actual findings still matter.","A real citation may not support the stated claim.","Confidence of wording is not verification."]},"med-case":{"event":"In the fictional transfer exercise, the responsible clinician has clarified the unexplained difference between the lists.","question":"What supports a clearer transition next?","answers":["Keep the clarification in memory.","Update the appropriate record, communicate the agreed plan and check understanding within your role.","Leave both lists unchanged so nobody loses information."],"correct":1,"rationales":["Memory alone does not communicate a resolved plan to others.","The resolution should reach the approved record, receiving team and patient through the appropriate workflow.","Unresolved conflicting records can allow the discrepancy to recur."]}};
const HCA_ACTIVITIES=[{"id":"handover","type":"scenarios","title":"The handover that almost missed it","desc":"During a simulated shift handover, a colleague describes a patient as “stable.” You notice newly documented deterioration and the review plan is unclear. You are concerned.","meta":"Scenario · 3 decisions · About 4 min","image":"activity-handover","action":"startCase('handover')","icon":"dialogue"},{"id":"teachback","type":"scenarios","title":"“Yes, I understand.” But do they?","desc":"In a simulated discharge conversation, a patient agrees with every explanation but cannot describe the follow-up plan. You have limited time.","meta":"Scenario · 3 decisions · About 4 min","image":"activity-teachback","action":"startCase('teachback')","icon":"dialogue"},{"id":"ai-case","type":"scenarios","title":"The confident answer that needs a check","desc":"In a fictional training exercise, an AI tool produces a polished summary with a clinical claim and a reference you do not recognize.","meta":"Scenario · 3 decisions · About 4 min","image":"activity-ai-case","action":"startCase('ai-case')","icon":"dialogue"},{"id":"med-case","type":"scenarios","title":"Two lists. One important pause.","desc":"In a simulated transfer, a medication appears on one list but not another. The reason for the difference is not documented.","meta":"Scenario · 3 decisions · About 4 min","image":"activity-med-case","action":"startCase('med-case')","icon":"dialogue"},{"id":"room","type":"studios","title":"The room. The pause. The decision.","desc":"Rotate an illustrated care room, explore five zones and reason through six hand-hygiene moments.","meta":"3D illustration · Six vignettes","action":"hcaOpenRoom()","icon":"room","image":"activity-room"},{"id":"hygiene","type":"studios","title":"The moments that matter","desc":"Explore six hand-hygiene decisions with an animated scene, contextual hotspots and immediate feedback.","meta":"Interactive Studio · Six decisions","action":"hcaLaunchStudio('hygiene')","image":"activity-hygiene","icon":"spark"},{"id":"sbar-studio","type":"studios","title":"Pass the message clearly","desc":"Arrange the message, check the structure and confirm how the next action will happen.","meta":"Interactive Studio · SBAR sequencing","action":"hcaLaunchStudio('sbar')","image":"activity-sbar-studio","icon":"dialogue"},{"id":"movement","type":"studios","title":"A little space to move","desc":"Watch three optional seated movement demonstrations. Pause, slow down or step through the illustration.","meta":"Wellbeing Studio · Three demonstrations","action":"hcaLaunchStudio('movement')","image":"activity-movement","icon":"spark"},{"id":"cus","type":"team","title":"Speak up with clarity","desc":"Name a concern, make the uncertainty explicit and ask for a useful response.","meta":"Team practice · CUS conversation","action":"openAdv('cus')","icon":"dialogue","image":"activity-cus"},{"id":"huddle","type":"team","title":"Bring the team into alignment","desc":"Identify named ownership, respond to changed conditions and learn from the shared plan.","meta":"Team practice · Brief, huddle, debrief","action":"openAdv('huddle')","icon":"team","image":"activity-huddle"},{"id":"flow","type":"team","title":"Test a change. Watch the flow.","desc":"Adjust a transparent fictional process, compare two capacities and explain what the model can show.","meta":"QI sandbox · Synthetic process model","action":"openAdv('flow')","icon":"growth","image":"activity-flow"},{"id":"cc-teach","type":"studios","title":"Build the teach-back loop","desc":"Sequence a conversation from a manageable explanation to a repaired understanding gap.","meta":"Interactive Studio · Sequence & explain","action":"ccOpenStudio('cc-teach')","icon":"dialogue","image":"activity-cc-teach"},{"id":"cc-risk","type":"studios","title":"See the absolute difference","desc":"Move two fictional risks and compare an absolute difference with a relative change.","meta":"Interactive Studio · Numbers & visual model","action":"ccOpenStudio('cc-risk')","icon":"growth","image":"activity-cc-risk"},{"id":"cc-reconcile","type":"studios","title":"Spot the transfer discrepancy","desc":"Compare two fictional lists, classify the differences and route the unresolved questions.","meta":"Interactive Studio · Compare & classify","action":"ccOpenStudio('cc-reconcile')","icon":"tools","image":"activity-cc-reconcile"},{"id":"cc-bias","type":"studios","title":"Catch the first-story trap","desc":"Work through three reasoning moments and seek information that could challenge an assumption.","meta":"Interactive Studio · Three decisions","action":"ccOpenStudio('cc-bias')","icon":"dialogue","image":"activity-cc-bias"},{"id":"cc-verify","type":"studios","title":"Trace the AI claim","desc":"Inspect a fictional citation, its population and a proposed sharing workflow.","meta":"Interactive Studio · Three decisions","action":"ccOpenStudio('cc-verify')","icon":"dialogue","image":"activity-cc-verify"},{"id":"cc-access","type":"studios","title":"Make participation possible","desc":"Spot an access barrier, ask the person and adapt the interaction.","meta":"Interactive Studio · Three decisions","action":"ccOpenStudio('cc-access')","icon":"dialogue","image":"activity-cc-access"},{"id":"cc-interrupt","type":"studios","title":"Recover after an interruption","desc":"Build a deliberate return to a fictional administrative task after competing demands.","meta":"Interactive Studio · Sequence & explain","action":"ccOpenStudio('cc-interrupt')","icon":"dialogue","image":"activity-cc-interrupt"},{"id":"cc-compassion","type":"studios","title":"Support with warmth and limits","desc":"Practise empathetic responses, honest boundaries and a practical support route.","meta":"Interactive Studio · Three decisions","action":"ccOpenStudio('cc-compassion')","icon":"dialogue","image":"activity-cc-compassion"},{"id":"sim-handover","type":"simulations","title":"The handover control room","desc":"Explore a 3D room, locate three communication gaps and make the next action explicit.","meta":"3D simulation · Three interactive checkpoints","action":"ccOpenSimulation('sim-handover')","image":"generated-sim-handover","icon":"room"},{"id":"sim-interruptions","type":"simulations","title":"The interruption recovery desk","desc":"Inspect a task desk, distinguish competing demands and recover a verified task state.","meta":"3D simulation · Three interactive checkpoints","action":"ccOpenSimulation('sim-interruptions')","image":"generated-sim-interruptions","icon":"room"},{"id":"sim-shared-choice","type":"simulations","title":"The shared-choice consultation","desc":"Explore a consultation space, compare fictional numbers and bring the person’s priorities into the plan.","meta":"3D simulation · Three interactive checkpoints","action":"ccOpenSimulation('sim-shared-choice')","image":"generated-sim-shared-choice","icon":"room"}];
const CC_SECTIONS={"dashboard":["Your next useful step","A workspace for resuming, choosing practice and returning to a review.","A clear next action reduces the effort of finding a useful activity.","At the beginning of a learning session.","Choose your focus and available time; resume or select the suggested activity.","Any practitioner; use Preferences to describe your role.","harm"],"library":["Learn: three connected dimensions","Thirty lessons connect Knowledge, Skills and Attitudes.","Understanding, practical action and professional habits need to develop together.","When you want a principle, a worked example and a recall check.","Filter a dimension, open a lesson, attempt a check and save a next action.","Practitioners, learners and educators across care settings.","harm"],"knowledge":["Knowledge: understand the reason","Ten foundations in safety, evidence, human factors and digital practice.","A useful principle helps you explain why a particular action matters.","Before practice or when an assumption needs examination.","Read a principle, compare the worked example, then answer a recall prompt.","Any practitioner working within their own role.","harm"],"skills":["Skills: rehearse the action","Ten practical topics for communication, documentation, feedback and team learning.","Specific rehearsal helps turn an intention into an observable next step.","Before a difficult conversation or when refining a routine.","Practise with fictional material, inspect feedback and try a different response.","Individuals, peer pairs and facilitated learning groups.","handoff"],"attitudes":["Attitudes: shape the response","Ten lessons on empathy, humility, fairness, dignity and sustainable practice.","How a concern is received can influence whether the next voice is heard.","When reflecting on a decision or the way people are treated.","Compare responses, question an assumption and choose one behaviour to practise.","Every team member, including people who lead or supervise.","access"],"pathways":["Learning pathways","Three routes organise ten lessons per dimension.","A route can make an extended learning goal easier to navigate.","When choosing a longer sequence rather than one short lesson.","Start with your confidence reflection or any dimension, then revisit your progress.","Self-directed learners and educators planning a session.","harm"],"practice":["Interactive practice","Scenarios, Studios and team exercises with feedback and preserved attempts.","Practice makes reasoning visible in a fictional setting.","When you want to try a response before using it in your own role.","Choose a format, make a decision, read the debrief and save the completed attempt.","Individual learners, peer pairs and facilitated groups.","simulation"],"studios":["Interactive Studios","Visual workspaces for sorting, calculating, comparing and rehearsing.","Different tasks benefit from different kinds of interaction.","When you want to test a principle actively rather than read about it.","Choose an activity, follow its instructions, review feedback and save your practice.","Any learner; all new activities work with buttons and keyboard controls.","simulation"],"team":["Teams & improvement","Exercises for speaking up, team alignment and process testing.","Shared ownership and visible constraints make a team plan easier to act on.","For a fictional team rehearsal or planning an approved improvement.","Try a CUS response, explore a huddle or inspect the transparent flow model.","Team members, facilitators and improvement leads.","harm"],"tools":["Toolkit: make a principle usable","Editable prompts for handovers, understanding, reflection and improvement.","A structured prompt can expose an omitted action or unanswered question.","When drafting a fictional rehearsal or a non-confidential learning plan.","Open a tool, compare examples, edit your draft and keep a private backup.","Practitioners and educators; approved local tools govern actual care.","audit"],"progress":["My Growth: a learning record","A history of attempts, reflections, actions and due recall reviews.","Revisiting a decision helps you notice what you can now explain or do differently.","After practice and when returning to a planned review.","Read an attempt, review your action, export a record or create a backup.","The learner using this browser; the record is not certification.","harm"],"saved":["Your saved collection","Bookmarks for lessons you want to revisit.","Saving creates a personal route through a larger catalogue.","When a topic is relevant but you cannot explore it now.","Use the bookmark on a lesson; return here to resume it.","Anyone building a personal learning collection.","harm"],"search":["Find a useful activity","Search across lessons, scenarios, tools, terms and Studios.","A concrete question is often a useful starting point.","When you know a topic or skill you need to explore.","Search a keyword such as teach-back, risk, dignity or medication.","Learners and educators finding a relevant activity.","harm"]};
const CC_STUDIOS=[{"id":"cc-teach","title":"Build the teach-back loop","description":"Sequence a conversation from a manageable explanation to a repaired understanding gap.","mode":"sequence","evidence":"teachback","lesson":"teachback-skill","why":"A check becomes useful when an explanation gap leads to repair.","when":"Before a follow-up or information conversation.","how":"Select the five moves in order, check your sequence and read the feedback.","steps":["Explain one next step in plain language.","Invite the person to explain it in their own words.","Listen and identify the understanding gap.","Re-explain the unclear part in a different way.","Invite teach-back again to check the repair."],"intro":"The person describes a different next step from the one intended. Build the full loop, including the repair."},{"id":"cc-risk","title":"See the absolute difference","description":"Move two fictional risks and compare an absolute difference with a relative change.","mode":"risk","evidence":"engagement","lesson":"risk-evidence","why":"Relative percentages can sound large without revealing a baseline.","when":"When preparing to explain a study or compare two options.","how":"Adjust the fictional figures, calculate the change, and compare your answer with the visual model."},{"id":"cc-reconcile","title":"Spot the transfer discrepancy","description":"Compare two fictional lists, classify the differences and route the unresolved questions.","mode":"match","evidence":"medication","lesson":"med-transitions","why":"A list mismatch must be verified rather than turned into an assumed prescription.","when":"Before practising an approved reconciliation workflow.","how":"Compare the records and classify each row; inspect the debrief before saving.","rows":[["Medicine A","Listed as current","Not listed","Omission / status to verify"],["Medicine B","Listed once","Listed twice","Possible duplicate to verify"],["Medicine C","Listed as current","Listed as current","No visible discrepancy in these records"]],"options":["Omission / status to verify","Possible duplicate to verify","No visible discrepancy in these records"]},{"id":"cc-bias","title":"Catch the first-story trap","description":"Work through three reasoning moments and seek information that could challenge an assumption.","mode":"decisions","evidence":"harm","lesson":"humility","why":"A plausible first explanation can obscure a mismatch.","when":"During a fictional case review or peer discussion.","how":"Choose a response at each moment and compare the reasons for all options.","scenes":[["The early label","A fictional team has an early explanation. A new observation does not fit.","What should happen next?",["Name the mismatch and revisit alternatives with the responsible team.","Set the new information aside.","Ask only for evidence confirming the first explanation."],0,["The mismatch should inform reassessment and appropriate review.","This preserves the first explanation without examining new evidence.","Confirming evidence alone can reinforce an incomplete explanation."]],["The confident colleague","A senior colleague sounds certain, but the relevant source has not been checked.","What makes the discussion more useful?",["Accept certainty as proof.","Ask which observations support the view and what would change it.","Wait until no uncertainty remains before discussing it."],1,["Confidence alone does not establish a claim.","This invites the evidence and its limits into the conversation.","Uncertainty can be communicated and managed now."]],["The updated view","Review changes the fictional team’s working explanation.","What closes the loop?",["Keep the update among the people in the room.","Treat the earlier uncertainty as a personal failure.","Communicate the revised view and next action to the relevant people."],2,["Relevant others may still be working with the earlier plan.","Updating a view is part of careful reasoning.","An updated explanation must reach those who need to act."]]]},{"id":"cc-verify","title":"Trace the AI claim","description":"Inspect a fictional citation, its population and a proposed sharing workflow.","mode":"decisions","evidence":"digital","lesson":"data-stewardship","why":"A fluent claim can still be unsupported, irrelevant or unsafe to share.","when":"Before relying on digital material in an approved workflow.","how":"Choose what to check at each stage, then inspect your complete decision trail.","scenes":[["A polished reference","A fictional AI draft names an unfamiliar study but gives no retrievable source.","What is the useful next check?",["Locate the actual publication and inspect the claim independently.","Ask the same model to promise accuracy.","Treat the citation’s formatting as verification."],0,["Independent checking tests whether the source and support exist.","Reassurance from the same system is not independent evidence.","Presentation does not establish that a reference is real."]],["A different population","You locate a real paper. Its participants and outcome differ from the draft.","What should change?",["Use it because the journal is real.","Do not generalise the claim; examine relevance and seek appropriate review.","Remove the population details to simplify the claim."],1,["A real paper may not support this particular claim.","Relevance is necessary in addition to source existence.","Removing context makes the claim harder to assess."]],["An unsafe shortcut","A colleague suggests uploading a note without its name to an unapproved public tool.","What is appropriate?",["Upload it if the answer is useful.","Assume removing a name makes it anonymous.","Use the approved information-governance workflow; other details may identify someone."],2,["Usefulness does not authorise disclosure.","Other details and combinations can identify someone.","Purpose, authorisation and the full information content matter."]]]},{"id":"cc-access","title":"Make participation possible","description":"Spot an access barrier, ask the person and adapt the interaction.","mode":"decisions","evidence":"access","lesson":"dignity","why":"The usual format may create a barrier that is not immediately visible.","when":"When preparing a conversation, appointment or learning session.","how":"Explore three fictional moments, choose an adaptation and review the reasoning.","scenes":[["A quiet agreement","A fictional person nods during an unfamiliar-language explanation.","What is the useful next step?",["Ask about preferred language and arrange appropriate support.","Assume the nod confirms understanding.","Ask a nearby child to translate clinical details."],0,["A direct preference check and appropriate support improve access.","Agreement alone does not reveal understanding.","Use qualified interpretation through the service where needed."]],["An inaccessible format","The person says the standard written sheet is difficult to use.","How should you respond?",["Give a second copy of the same sheet.","Ask what format or support works for them and arrange it through the service.","Infer the person’s ability from appearance."],1,["Repeating the same format may repeat the barrier.","The person’s expressed needs should shape the adaptation.","Appearance cannot establish what support is useful."]],["A companion speaks","A companion answers everything while the person is present.","What supports participation?",["Address all questions only to the companion.","Assume the person does not want involvement.","Speak directly to the person and ask how they want the companion involved."],2,["This can leave the person’s own voice unheard.","Ask rather than assume their preference.","Direct address and a preference check preserve participation."]]]},{"id":"cc-interrupt","title":"Recover after an interruption","description":"Build a deliberate return to a fictional administrative task after competing demands.","mode":"sequence","evidence":"interruptions","lesson":"human-factors","why":"An interruption can leave task status and responsibility unclear.","when":"When reflecting on interruptions in non-clinical learning work.","how":"Sequence the four recovery moves, then consider which system support would help.","intro":"Fictional task: preparing a learning pack. An interruption leaves you unsure which checks were completed. This sequence is not a clinical procedure.","steps":["Pause and identify the task’s current status.","Verify completed and outstanding steps against the approved task record.","Clarify uncertainty or ownership before continuing.","Resume from the verified point and record the next action."]},{"id":"cc-compassion","title":"Support with warmth and limits","description":"Practise empathetic responses, honest boundaries and a practical support route.","mode":"decisions","evidence":"wellbeing","lesson":"compassion-boundaries","why":"Support is more dependable when it acknowledges the person and your role.","when":"Before a peer-support or workload conversation.","how":"Choose a response in three moments, then review the action and the boundary.","scenes":[["The first response","A fictional colleague says the workload feels unmanageable.","Which response opens a useful discussion?",["That sounds difficult. What is the most pressing concern right now?","Everyone is busy.","A one-minute reset will solve it."],0,["Acknowledgment and an open question invite the person’s actual concern.","Comparison can close the discussion.","A short reset does not resolve continuing workload conditions."]],["A realistic offer","You can listen now but cannot change the rota yourself.","What can you say?",["I guarantee your workload will change.","I can listen and help identify the appropriate person to discuss the workload.","I will take sole responsibility for fixing everything."],1,["Avoid promising an outcome outside your control.","This combines a useful action with an honest boundary.","An unlimited promise can create an unreliable support plan."]],["Beyond a short pause","The strain is persistent and affecting concentration.","What should the response include?",["Only another breathing exercise.","Keeping it private in every circumstance.","Appropriate support and a workload or safety discussion through the local route."],2,["Continuing strain may need more than an immediate pause.","Appropriate trusted support may be important.","A response should consider the person and working conditions."]]]}];
const CC_EVIDENCE={"harm":{"title":"Preventable harm across care settings","numbers":[["6%","pooled prevalence of preventable harm"],["70","studies; 337,025 patients"]],"kind":"bar","ratio":6,"source":"Panagioti et al. · BMJ · 2019","url":"https://www.bmj.com/content/366/bmj.l4185","type":"Systematic review","finding":"Preventable harm was reported across multiple care settings.","population":"70 observational studies; 337,025 patients across medical care settings.","context":"Pooled estimate 6% (95% CI 5–7%). Rates and definitions varied between studies. This is historical synthesis, not a local incident rate or Carecraft outcome."},"handoff":{"title":"A structured handoff programme","numbers":[["23%","relative reduction in medical errors"],["30%","relative reduction in preventable adverse events"]],"kind":"bar","ratio":23,"source":"Starmer et al. · NEJM · 2014","url":"https://www.nejm.org/doi/abs/10.1056/NEJMsa1405556","type":"Multicentre before-and-after study","finding":"Error rates fell after implementation of the I-PASS bundle.","population":"Nine paediatric residency programmes; 10,740 patient admissions.","context":"A multicomponent intervention, not an SBAR-only effect. Before-and-after associations do not isolate every cause or predict this app’s effect."},"medication":{"title":"Medication reconciliation at transitions","numbers":[["2.85","discrepancies per patient at the start"],["0.98","discrepancies per patient at the end"]],"kind":"compare","ratio":34.3859649122807,"source":"Schnipper et al. · BMJ Quality & Safety · 2022","url":"https://qualitysafety.bmj.com/content/31/4/278","type":"Multicentre quality-improvement study","finding":"Discrepancy rates fell during the MARQUIS2 implementation.","population":"18 North American hospitals or hospital systems, with data collection at 17; 2016–2018.","context":"Observed rates across an 18-month, mentored, multicomponent intervention. A discrepancy is not synonymous with injury. No individual tool effect or dose decision is inferred."},"ipc":{"title":"A bundle for catheter-related infection","numbers":[["Up to 66%","reduction in infection rates"],["103","ICUs reporting data"]],"kind":"bar","ratio":66,"source":"Pronovost et al. · NEJM · 2006","url":"https://www.nejm.org/doi/abs/10.1056/NEJMoa061115","type":"Collaborative cohort study","finding":"A coordinated infection-prevention programme was associated with sustained improvement.","population":"Michigan intensive-care units; catheter-related bloodstream infections; 18-month study.","context":"Historical, multicomponent ICU programme. This is not the effect of hand hygiene alone, a general infection rate, or proof that a Studio prevents infections."},"access":{"title":"Accessibility is a routine need","numbers":[["28.7%","of US adults reported functional disability"],["~70m","US adults, based on 2022 data"]],"kind":"bar","ratio":28.7,"source":"CDC · Disability and Health Data Now · 2025","url":"https://www.cdc.gov/disability-and-health/articles-documents/disability-and-health-data-now.html","type":"Population surveillance","finding":"Accessible services matter to a substantial part of the adult population.","population":"2022 US Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance System, reported by CDC.","context":"US self-reported survey data, not global or Saudi prevalence. Definitions differ from other surveys. The figure does not reveal any individual’s needs: ask the person."},"wellbeing":{"title":"Burnout and reported safety incidents","numbers":[["2.04","odds ratio for patient-safety incidents"],["170","studies in the full review"]],"kind":"none","source":"Hodkinson et al. · BMJ · 2022","url":"https://www.bmj.com/content/378/bmj-2022-070442","type":"Observational meta-analysis","finding":"Physician burnout was associated with higher odds of safety incidents.","population":"239,246 physicians in the full review. The overall-burnout/safety analysis used 35 studies and 41,059 physicians.","context":"OR 2.04 (95% CI 1.69–2.45); substantial heterogeneity. Odds are not risk. Association does not establish causation or the effect of an individual wellbeing exercise."},"engagement":{"title":"Decision aids improve understanding","numbers":[["+11.9","points on a 100-point knowledge scale"],["209","studies in the complete review"]],"kind":"none","source":"Stacey et al. · Cochrane · 2024","url":"https://www.cochrane.org/evidence/CD001431_patient-decision-aids-help-people-who-are-facing-decisions-about-health-treatment-or-screening","type":"Review of randomised trials","finding":"Decision aids improved knowledge compared with usual care.","population":"107,698 adults in the full review. The knowledge estimate pooled 107 studies and 25,492 participants.","context":"Mean difference 11.90/100 (95% CI 10.60–13.19), high-certainty evidence for this outcome. Evidence searched to March 2022; aids supplement clinical consultation."},"teachback":{"title":"Teach-back across different settings","numbers":[["20","studies included"],["19","reported benefit on at least one outcome"]],"kind":"none","source":"Talevski et al. · PLOS ONE · 2020","url":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0231350","type":"Systematic review","finding":"Teach-back showed promising results, with varied methods and outcomes.","population":"Studies across different populations and care settings, searched to July 2019.","context":"Counts of studies, not a 95% treatment benefit. Seven studies were rated weak and outcomes were heterogeneous; no single pooled clinical effect is claimed."},"empathy":{"title":"Empathy and positive communication","numbers":[["28","trials in the full review"],["6,017","participants overall"]],"kind":"none","source":"Howick et al. · Journal of the Royal Society of Medicine · 2018","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6047264/","type":"Review of randomised trials","finding":"The empathy subgroup found a small improvement in pooled patient outcomes.","population":"Seven trials tested empathic consultations; other trials tested positive expectations.","context":"Empathy subgroup SMD −0.18 (95% CI −0.32 to −0.03). This standardised effect is not a percentage reduction. Conditions, outcomes and certainty varied."},"interruptions":{"title":"Interruptions during medication work","numbers":[["53.1%","of observed administrations were interrupted"],["4,271","medication administrations observed"]],"kind":"bar","ratio":53.1,"source":"Westbrook et al. · Archives of Internal Medicine · 2010","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20421552/","type":"Direct observational study","finding":"More interruptions were associated with more errors in the observed setting.","population":"98 nurses, six wards, two Sydney teaching hospitals; 2006–2008 observations.","context":"Each interruption was associated with a 12.7% increase in clinical errors in the study model. Observational association, not a universal error probability or a rationale to delay urgent communication."},"digital":{"title":"A citation can look real and still be false","numbers":[["55%","fabricated citations in the GPT-3.5 sample"],["18%","fabricated citations in the GPT-4 sample"]],"kind":"compare","ratio":32.72727272727273,"source":"Walters & Wilder · Scientific Reports · 2023","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-41032-5","type":"Model-output benchmark study","finding":"The sampled outputs contained fabricated and erroneous references.","population":"636 citations generated for the study’s prompts using 2023-era models.","context":"Historical benchmark, not a current error rate for all AI systems or clinical outputs. Different prompts, versions and verification workflows can produce different results."},"simulation":{"title":"Why actively rehearse?","numbers":[["609","studies included"],["35,226","health-professions trainees"]],"kind":"none","source":"Cook et al. · JAMA · 2011","url":"https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/article-abstract/1104300","type":"Systematic review and meta-analysis","finding":"Simulation training outperformed no intervention on pooled learning outcomes.","population":"Multiple health professions and simulation formats; 137 randomised studies plus other designs.","context":"Large heterogeneity and predominantly pre/post studies. The comparison was no intervention; it does not establish superiority to every teaching method or validate these particular simulations."},"audit":{"title":"Feedback can support better practice","numbers":[["+6.2","percentage-point mean improvement"],["292","studies in the full review"]],"kind":"none","source":"Ivers et al. · Cochrane · 2025","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40130784/","type":"Systematic review","finding":"Audit and feedback improved desired professional practice on average.","population":"The mean estimate pooled 177 studies comparing audit and feedback with control.","context":"95% CI 4.1–8.2 percentage points; moderate-certainty evidence. Effects varied and interventions often included education or reminders. This is not an effect of informal feedback alone."}};
const CC_TOPIC_EVIDENCE={"communication":"teachback","safety":"medication","empathy":"empathy","sbar":"handoff","infection":"ipc","reasoning":"harm","speakup":"handoff","shared":"engagement","ai":"digital","wellbeing":"wellbeing","equity":"access","improvement":"audit","health-literacy":"teachback","risk-evidence":"engagement","human-factors":"interruptions","med-transitions":"medication","deterioration":"handoff","data-stewardship":"digital","teachback-skill":"teachback","conflict":"empathy","documentation":"handoff","risk-dialogue":"engagement","feedback":"audit","debrief":"simulation","humility":"harm","dignity":"access","just-culture":"harm","accountability":"handoff","compassion-boundaries":"wellbeing","inclusive-leadership":"access"};
const CC_SIMS=[{"id":"sim-handover","title":"The handover control room","image":"handover","lesson":"sbar","evidence":"handoff","theme":"bedside","description":"Explore a 3D room, locate three communication gaps and make the next action explicit.","why":"A handover needs a shared action, named responsibility and a contingency.","when":"Before practising a handover or facilitating a team rehearsal.","how":"Explore all three hotspots, choose a response at each and review your decision trail.","zones":[{"name":"The bedside change","label":"Observe","x":102,"y":135,"copy":"The fictional handover says “stable”, but the new observation in the scenario does not fit that label. No patient measurements or treatment decisions are simulated.","question":"What should the message lead with?","answers":["The observed change and your concern, with urgency communicated through the local route.","The earlier reassuring label, without mentioning the change.","A complete historical narrative before any concern."],"correct":0,"why":["A current change should be explicit. Urgent concerns require the immediate local pathway.","An earlier label does not resolve new information.","Relevant background matters, but must not obscure or delay a significant concern."]},{"name":"The responsibility board","label":"Assign","x":305,"y":78,"copy":"The fictional receiving team has acknowledged the concern. The review task still reads “team aware” and has no named owner.","question":"What remains to be agreed?","answers":["That the message was long enough.","Who will act, the agreed timing and how responsibility is accepted.","That everyone in the room has received a copy."],"correct":1,"why":["Length does not establish ownership.","An explicit owner and timing turn acknowledgment into an actionable plan.","More recipients can still leave responsibility unassigned."]},{"name":"The communication station","label":"Close","x":290,"y":205,"copy":"A named person has accepted the action. The fictional team has not agreed what happens if the review is delayed or the situation changes.","question":"Which closing action is useful?","answers":["Assume the original plan will happen.","Document the concern and end the discussion.","Confirm the contingency and local re-escalation route, alongside the approved record."],"correct":2,"why":["A plan also needs a response to delay or change.","Documentation supports communication but does not establish the contingency.","Closing the loop includes what to do when the plan cannot be carried out."]}]},{"id":"sim-interruptions","title":"The interruption recovery desk","image":"interruptions","lesson":"human-factors","evidence":"interruptions","theme":"desk","description":"Inspect a task desk, distinguish competing demands and recover a verified task state.","why":"An interruption can leave task status, urgency and ownership uncertain.","when":"When reviewing interruption-prone work or planning a safer task handoff.","how":"Inspect the task record, incoming demand and return point. Choose a response at each hotspot.","zones":[{"name":"The task record","label":"Verify","x":170,"y":95,"copy":"A fictional coordination task was interrupted. A checklist is open, but you do not remember which parts were completed. This is a communication exercise, not medication preparation.","question":"What is the useful starting point?","answers":["Resume where you think you were.","Check the approved task record and establish completed and outstanding steps.","Mark the task complete to prevent duplication."],"correct":1,"why":["Memory alone may not reveal the actual task state.","A verified task state is a stronger basis for resuming than an assumption.","Unverified completion can hide outstanding work."]},{"name":"The incoming demand","label":"Prioritise","x":318,"y":170,"copy":"A colleague interrupts with a request. The scenario does not establish whether the request is urgent. “Never interrupt” would be an unsafe blanket rule.","question":"What should you clarify?","answers":["The request’s urgency and appropriate response, using local escalation where indicated.","Whether the colleague can wait in every circumstance.","Whether ignoring requests will make the queue smaller."],"correct":0,"why":["Urgent concerns must not be blocked by a task-protection habit. Clarify the actual need.","Some requests require immediate attention.","Silence does not resolve urgency or responsibility."]},{"name":"The return tray","label":"Resume","x":90,"y":208,"copy":"You have addressed the incoming demand. A second person may have moved the original task forward while you were away.","question":"How do you avoid a silent gap or duplicate work?","answers":["Restart every step regardless of what others did.","Continue from the original memory of your place.","Confirm the current status and owner, then resume from the verified point."],"correct":2,"why":["A full restart may introduce unnecessary duplication; first establish the state.","The task may have changed while you were away.","A status-and-ownership check supports a deliberate return."]}]},{"id":"sim-shared-choice","title":"The shared-choice consultation","image":"shared-decision","lesson":"shared","evidence":"engagement","theme":"conversation","description":"Explore a consultation space, compare fictional numbers and bring the person’s priorities into the plan.","why":"A balanced comparison needs evidence, uncertainty and the person’s practical priorities.","when":"Before rehearsing a shared-decision or risk-explanation conversation.","how":"Explore the person’s priorities, the number board and the follow-up plan. The displayed numbers are synthetic.","zones":[{"name":"The person’s priorities","label":"Ask","x":82,"y":155,"copy":"In a fictional conversation, two possible plans have different visit and preparation burdens. The person has not been asked what would be difficult or important to them.","question":"Which question makes the comparison more useful?","answers":["Which plan does the team prefer?","What matters most to you, and what would make either plan difficult?","Which option is most popular?"],"correct":1,"why":["Team preference alone does not establish the person’s priorities.","A preference-and-barrier question helps connect options to the individual.","A popular option may not fit this person."]},{"name":"The fictional number board","label":"Compare","x":200,"y":72,"copy":"Synthetic example: the same outcome occurs in 8 of 100 people with one option and 5 of 100 with another, over the same period. No treatment, real-world rate or causal claim is implied.","question":"Which explanation keeps the absolute difference visible?","answers":["That is 3 fewer in 100, or 3 percentage points, alongside burdens and uncertainty.","That is 37.5 fewer people in every 100.","The second option guarantees a better outcome for this person."],"correct":0,"why":["8/100 minus 5/100 is 3/100. The baseline, time period and trade-offs remain important.","37.5% is the relative reduction in this synthetic example, not the absolute number in 100.","A group-level probability does not guarantee an individual result."]},{"name":"The shared next step","label":"Agree","x":310,"y":180,"copy":"The person wants time to consider the options and discuss a practical barrier. The plan is not yet agreed.","question":"What supports an informed next step?","answers":["Record agreement because the information has been provided.","Choose the option with the larger-looking percentage.","Clarify the support needed and agree appropriate follow-up with the responsible clinician."],"correct":2,"why":["Receiving information is not the same as reaching a shared agreement.","A percentage alone cannot resolve priorities and uncertainty.","Support, ownership and follow-up help the decision remain informed and revisable."]}]}];
const CC_USE={"communication":["Anyone explaining care or a next step.","Before an explanation, discharge conversation or follow-up discussion."],"safety":["Care practitioners and teams managing transitions.","When a record, instruction or intended action is unclear."],"empathy":["Anyone speaking with a person about their care.","When a concern, emotion or practical barrier needs to be understood."],"sbar":["Colleagues transferring information and responsibility.","At a handover or when raising a change or unresolved concern."],"infection":["People involved in care and shared equipment.","When planning precautions or moving between care tasks."],"reasoning":["Practitioners and learners reviewing an explanation.","When new information does not fit the first interpretation."],"speakup":["Every team member, including students and supervisors.","When raising a concern or receiving a colleague’s question."],"shared":["Care teams and people considering genuine options.","When options involve different benefits, burdens or priorities."],"ai":["Staff and learners using approved digital workflows.","Before relying on a generated claim, summary or citation."],"wellbeing":["Healthcare workers, peers and supervisors.","When strain, recovery or working conditions need attention."],"equity":["Teams planning an accessible conversation or service.","When the usual format may not meet a person’s needs."],"improvement":["Teams and facilitators planning an approved test.","When a recurring process gap needs a measurable change."],"health-literacy":["Anyone explaining information or service instructions.","When jargon, format or navigation makes a next step hard to use."],"risk-evidence":["People interpreting studies or explaining probabilities.","When a percentage is presented without its baseline or time period."],"human-factors":["People reviewing how work actually happens.","When interruptions, memory load or unclear cues make a task fragile."],"med-transitions":["Teams involved in medication reconciliation.","When medication accounts differ at a transfer or transition."],"deterioration":["Care teams practising recognition and escalation.","When a current change conflicts with an earlier reassuring label."],"data-stewardship":["Anyone handling confidential or generated information.","Before sharing material or choosing a digital destination."],"teachback-skill":["Practitioners and learners rehearsing an explanation.","When checking and repairing a gap in shared understanding."],"conflict":["Colleagues seeking a useful response to disagreement.","When personal friction is obscuring an observable concern."],"documentation":["People writing an actionable care or learning record.","When the next colleague needs clear facts, ownership and timing."],"risk-dialogue":["People rehearsing a balanced options conversation.","When numbers need to be connected to the person’s priorities."],"feedback":["Peers, supervisors, educators and learners.","When a specific observation can inform a manageable next step."],"debrief":["Teams and facilitators reflecting on a fictional event.","After a rehearsal, event or task with useful learning to discuss."],"humility":["People reviewing a working explanation or assumption.","When another perspective or new information could change the view."],"dignity":["Anyone participating in a care conversation.","When permission, privacy or the person’s voice needs attention."],"just-culture":["Teams reviewing a near miss or process failure.","When facts, context and fair accountability need to be considered together."],"accountability":["Anyone accepting, handing over or following up a task.","When a commitment needs an owner, timing or a contingency."],"compassion-boundaries":["Colleagues and practitioners offering practical support.","When a supportive response also needs an honest boundary."],"inclusive-leadership":["Team members, facilitators and people leading a discussion.","When a decision is missing a role, perspective or accessible route for input."]};
const CC_ART_ICONS={"communication":"<path d=\"M4 4h16v12H9l-5 4zM8 8h8M8 12h5\"/>","safety":"<path d=\"m12 2 8 3v6c0 6-8 11-8 11S4 17 4 11V5zM8 12l3 3 5-6\"/>","empathy":"<path d=\"M12 20 4 12C-1 7 6 1 12 7c6-6 13 0 8 5zM7 11h3l2-3 2 6 2-3h2\"/>","sbar":"<rect x=\"3\" y=\"3\" width=\"18\" height=\"18\" rx=\"3\"/><path d=\"M7 7h3M14 7h3M7 12h10M7 17h6\"/>","infection":"<path d=\"M12 2v4M12 18v4M2 12h4M18 12h4M5 5l3 3M16 16l3 3M5 19l3-3M16 8l3-3\"/><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"6\"/><path d=\"m9 12 2 2 4-4\"/>","reasoning":"<circle cx=\"10\" cy=\"10\" r=\"7\"/><path d=\"m15 15 6 6M7 10h6M10 7v6\"/>","speakup":"<path d=\"m3 10 13-5v14L3 14zM16 9l5-2v10l-5-2M5 15l2 6h4l-2-5M20 2l-2 2M22 20l-3-2\"/>","shared":"<circle cx=\"6\" cy=\"5\" r=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"18\" cy=\"5\" r=\"2\"/><path d=\"M2 15v-3a4 4 0 0 1 8 0v3M14 15v-3a4 4 0 0 1 8 0v3M7 19h10m-3-3 3 3-3 3\"/>","ai":"<rect x=\"4\" y=\"4\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" rx=\"4\"/><path d=\"M8 8h8v8H8zM8 1v3M16 1v3M8 20v3M16 20v3M1 8h3M1 16h3M20 8h3M20 16h3\"/>","wellbeing":"<path d=\"M3 20c1-10 6-15 18-16 0 12-5 17-15 16M3 22 16 9M9 16v-5M13 12h5\"/>","equity":"<path d=\"M3 20h18M5 20V8h5v12M14 20V4h5v16M5 4h5M14 1h5M3 12h9\"/>","improvement":"<path d=\"M19 6a9 9 0 1 0 2 10M19 2v6h-6M7 15l4-4 3 2 5-5\"/>","health-literacy":"<path d=\"M12 6v15M3 4h5l4 2 4-2h5v15h-5l-4 2-4-2H3zM6 8h2M6 12h2M16 8h2M16 12h2\"/>","risk-evidence":"<path d=\"M3 3v18h18M7 17v-5M12 17V7M17 17v-8M7 8l5-5 5 3\"/>","human-factors":"<circle cx=\"9\" cy=\"5\" r=\"3\"/><path d=\"M3 20v-4a6 6 0 0 1 10-4M16 11v3m0 6v3M10 17h3m6 0h3\"/><circle cx=\"16\" cy=\"17\" r=\"3\"/>","med-transitions":"<path d=\"m6 4 14 14M4 6a5 5 0 0 1 7-2l9 9a5 5 0 0 1-7 7l-9-9a5 5 0 0 1 0-7M2 20h7m-3-3 3 3-3 3\"/>","deterioration":"<path d=\"M2 12h5l3-8 4 16 3-8h5M4 3h3M17 21h3\"/>","data-stewardship":"<rect x=\"4\" y=\"10\" width=\"16\" height=\"12\" rx=\"2\"/><path d=\"M7 10V7a5 5 0 0 1 10 0v3M12 15v3\"/>","teachback-skill":"<path d=\"M4 7h14l-4-4M18 17H4l4 4M20 7v5M2 17v-5\"/><circle cx=\"11\" cy=\"12\" r=\"2\"/>","conflict":"<path d=\"M2 3h13v9H7l-5 4zM18 8h4v12l-5-4h-6v-2M6 7h5M17 12h2\"/>","documentation":"<path d=\"M5 2h10l4 4v16H5zM15 2v5h4M8 11h8M8 15h8M8 19h5\"/>","risk-dialogue":"<path d=\"M3 3h18v13H9l-6 5zM7 12V8M12 12V6M17 12V9\"/>","feedback":"<path d=\"M3 3h18v14H9l-6 5zM7 10l3 3 7-7\"/>","debrief":"<circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"4\"/><circle cx=\"5\" cy=\"4\" r=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"19\" cy=\"4\" r=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"21\" r=\"2\"/><path d=\"m7 6 2 3M17 6l-2 3M12 16v3\"/>","humility":"<path d=\"M7 5a8 8 0 1 1-2 11M7 1v6H1M10 10a2 2 0 1 1 3 2c-1 .5-1 1-1 2M12 17h.01\"/>","dignity":"<circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"6\" r=\"4\"/><path d=\"M4 22v-4a8 8 0 0 1 16 0v4M8 16l4 3 4-3\"/>","just-culture":"<path d=\"M12 2v20M5 22h14M3 6h18M5 6l-3 8h6zM19 6l-3 8h6z\"/>","accountability":"<rect x=\"4\" y=\"4\" width=\"16\" height=\"18\" rx=\"2\"/><path d=\"M9 2h6v4H9zM8 11l2 2 5-5M8 17h8\"/>","compassion-boundaries":"<path d=\"M3 3v18M21 3v18M12 18l-6-6c-4-4 2-8 6-3 4-5 10-1 6 3z\"/>","inclusive-leadership":"<circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"4\" r=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"4\" cy=\"12\" r=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"20\" cy=\"12\" r=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"20\" r=\"2\"/><path d=\"m6 6 3 3M15 9l3-3M6 18l3-3M15 15l3 3\"/>","room":"<path d=\"m3 6 9-4 9 4v12l-9 4-9-4zM3 6l9 5 9-5M12 11v11M6 15l3 1\"/>","movement":"<circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"4\" r=\"2\"/><path d=\"m5 10 7-3 7 3M12 7v7m0 0-5 7m5-7 5 7\"/>","flow":"<rect x=\"2\" y=\"3\" width=\"6\" height=\"6\" rx=\"1\"/><rect x=\"16\" y=\"15\" width=\"6\" height=\"6\" rx=\"1\"/><path d=\"M5 9v9h11M13 15l3 3-3 3M11 6h8v6M16 9l3 3 3-3\"/>","interruptions":"<path d=\"M4 3h11v18H4zM7 7h5M7 11h5M7 15h3M18 3v8M18 15h.01M16 21h6\"/>"};
const HCA_ROOM_ZONES=[["Patient zone","Patient contact and the immediate surroundings are distinct contexts. Use the vignette to identify what happened."],["Clean-task tray","A clean or aseptic task has its own hand-hygiene indication. The illustration does not demonstrate a procedure."],["Exposure-risk task","Assess the exposure described in the vignette. The markers do not detect or represent contamination."],["Bedside surroundings","Contact with patient surroundings can matter even when there has been no direct patient contact."],["Hand-hygiene station","Use the appropriate method under current local policy. Gloves do not replace indicated hand hygiene."]];
const CC_STATIC_CONTENT={"about":"<p>Carecraft connects 30 lessons, 90 practice prompts, four branching cases, expanded Interactive Studios and practical workspaces. Learn a principle, rehearse it and return for review.</p><div class=\"hca-how-grid\"><section><h3>Learn.</h3><p>Understand a principle through a clear explanation and a worked example.</p></section><section><h3>Practise.</h3><p>Explore a fictional decision, see its consequences and understand the alternatives.</p></section><section><h3>Return.</h3><p>Revisit a prompt, reflect on an action and keep a record of your learning.</p></section></div><h3>What your records mean</h3><p>Activity completion and self-reported reflections are not clinical competence, certification or accredited CPD/CME. Content has not undergone independent clinical peer review.</p><p>Created by Hossam AlDhaher, LSSMBB.</p><h3>One academy. A shared identity.</h3><p>The original academy crest family, navy, teal, copper and ivory palette, Playfair Display and Inter typography are used with the supplied Healthcare Crown Academy brand kit.</p><div class=\"hca-trust\"><a href=\"https://ico.org.uk/ESDWebPages/Entry/ZC273259\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><span class=\"hca-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"m12 3 8 3v6c0 5-8 10-8 10S4 17 4 12V6zM8 12l3 3 5-6\"></path></svg></span><span>ICO registration <b>ZC273259</b> ↗</span></a><a href=\"https://healthcarecrownacademy.co.uk\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Healthcare Crown Academy ↗</a></div><div class=\"modal-actions\"><button class=\"btn secondary\" data-action=\"engine-92\" data-engine-92=\"[]\">Download this app</button><button class=\"btn\" data-action=\"engine-93\" data-engine-93=\"[]\">Explore learning →</button></div>","privacy":"<p>This app saves your optional first name, role, learning goal, completed lessons, scenario activity, bookmarks, self-check, studio activities and reflections, and appearance preference in this browser’s local storage. It has no server-side account, cloud sync, or analytics.</p><h3>Use a trusted device</h3><p>Other people using the same browser profile may see your learning notes. Do not record patient, colleague, or confidential information. Clearing browser data removes your saved progress. Exported files stay on the device where you download them.</p><h3>External sources</h3><p>Source links open external websites under their own privacy policies. Images and fonts in the downloadable app are embedded, so the app does not need third-party asset requests.</p><div class=\"modal-actions\"><button class=\"btn secondary\" data-action=\"engine-94\" data-engine-94=\"[]\">Clear my saved data</button><button class=\"btn\" data-action=\"engine-28\" data-engine-28=\"[]\">Understood</button></div><h3>Your Phase 2 learning records</h3><p>Action plans, review dates and notes, lesson-check choices, fictional scenario trails, and tool drafts are also saved in this browser. Review prompts appear only inside the app when you return; no reminders or notifications are sent. The app does not validate clinical competence or verify your real-world actions.</p><h3>Carecraft version 2</h3><p>Your learning focus, setting, motion preference, latest session, recall review dates and individual Studio attempts are also stored in this browser. The review queue is shown only when you open the app; it sends no notifications. The current app has no analytics, accounts or cloud sync.</p><p>Backups contain your learning data and notes. Store them privately. A backup can restore your workspace; restoring replaces this browser’s existing Carecraft data.</p><div class=\"hca-backup-row\"><button class=\"btn secondary\" data-action=\"engine-95\" data-engine-95=\"[]\">Download backup</button><button class=\"btn secondary\" data-action=\"engine-96\" data-engine-96=\"[]\">Restore backup</button></div><p>Company: HEALTHCARE CROWN ACADEMY LTD, 71–75 Shelton Street, Covent Garden, London WC2H 9JQ. Company no. 17501904. ICO registration ZC273259, supplied in the company brand kit.</p>","sources":"<p>Lessons summarize established cross-disciplinary principles rather than specialty treatment protocols. Supporting links appear in each lesson. Source publication dates vary; this is not a claim that every source is newly published or that this app is continuously updated.</p><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>AHRQ · TeamSTEPPS 3.0 tools and demonstrations</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.ahrq.gov/teamstepps-program/resources/additional/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>AHRQ · SHARE: essential steps of shared decision-making</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.ahrq.gov/health-literacy/professional-training/shared-decision/tool/resource-1.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>WHO · Medication Without Harm</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.who.int/initiatives/medication-without-harm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>CDC · Standard and transmission-based precautions</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/infection-control/hcp/isolation-precautions/precautions.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>WHO · AI ethics and governance guidance (2024)</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news/item/18-01-2024-who-releases-ai-ethics-and-governance-guidance-for-large-multi-modal-models\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>WHO · Patient safety (2023)</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/patient-safety\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>AHRQ · Health Literacy Universal Precautions Toolkit, 3rd edition (2024)</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.ahrq.gov/health-literacy/improve/precautions/toolkit.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>WHO · Disability (2023)</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/disability-and-health\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>WHO · Mental health at work (2026)</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/mental-health-at-work\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Talevski et al. · PLOS ONE · 2020</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0231350\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Schnipper et al. · BMJ Quality &amp; Safety · 2022</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://qualitysafety.bmj.com/content/31/4/278\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Howick et al. · Journal of the Royal Society of Medicine · 2018</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6047264/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Starmer et al. · NEJM · 2014</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.nejm.org/doi/abs/10.1056/NEJMsa1405556\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Pronovost et al. · NEJM · 2006</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.nejm.org/doi/abs/10.1056/NEJMoa061115\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Panagioti et al. · BMJ · 2019</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.bmj.com/content/366/bmj.l4185\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Stacey et al. · Cochrane · 2024</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.cochrane.org/evidence/CD001431_patient-decision-aids-help-people-who-are-facing-decisions-about-health-treatment-or-screening\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Walters &amp; Wilder · Scientific Reports · 2023</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-41032-5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Hodkinson et al. · BMJ · 2022</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.bmj.com/content/378/bmj-2022-070442\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>CDC · Disability and Health Data Now · 2025</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/disability-and-health/articles-documents/disability-and-health-data-now.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Ivers et al. · Cochrane · 2025</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40130784/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Westbrook et al. · Archives of Internal Medicine · 2010</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20421552/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><div class=\"learning-step\"><div><strong>Cook et al. · JAMA · 2011</strong><p style=\"overflow-wrap:anywhere\"><a href=\"https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/article-abstract/1104300\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open source ↗</a></p></div></div><h3>Editorial transparency</h3><p>Clinical reasoning, quality-improvement, appraisal, and wellbeing sections include general educational synthesis. Scenario wording and quotes are original. No professional accreditation, independent clinical review, affiliation, or endorsement is implied.</p><h3>Image &amp; type credits</h3><p>The collaborative clinical-team image is AI-generated. Supporting photographic images are from Unsplash (image identifiers: 1576091160399-112ba8d25d1d, 1584982751601-97dcc096659c, 1472396961693-142e6e269027). DM Sans and Manrope are open-source typefaces supplied through Google Fonts. Images are illustrative, not evidence or depictions of actual participants.</p><div class=\"notice\" style=\"margin-top:18px\">Before using this app in formal staff education, obtain local clinical review, check applicable policies and source updates, and review accessibility, licensing, and governance requirements.</div><h3>Phase 2 editorial additions</h3><p>The worked examples, answer explanations, branching scenarios, and planning aids are original fictional educational material. They have not undergone independent clinical peer review. Supporting principles include AHRQ SBAR, CDC standard precautions, and WHO AI governance guidance. The handover photograph was AI-generated for this app and depicts fictional people.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/infection-control/hcp/basics/standard-precautions.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CDC: Standard Precautions for All Patient Care ↗</a></p><h3>Interactive expansion references</h3><p>Public image and web searches informed the choice of familiar learning patterns: scenario decisions, message confirmation, structured team boards, and improvement templates. Carecraft’s layout, examples, animation, and code are original. No third-party screenshots, illustrations, videos, logos, or proprietary game assets are embedded.</p><div class=\"adv-source\">Original Carecraft educational activity. Inspired by the structure of <a href=\"https://www.ahrq.gov/teamstepps-program/curriculum/mutual/tools/cus.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AHRQ CUS ↗</a>. No affiliation, endorsement, or independently validated score is implied.</div><div class=\"adv-source\">Original Carecraft educational activity. Inspired by the structure of <a href=\"https://www.ahrq.gov/teamstepps-program/curriculum/mutual/tools/desc.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AHRQ DESC ↗</a>. No affiliation, endorsement, or independently validated score is implied.</div><div class=\"adv-source\">Original Carecraft educational activity. Inspired by the structure of <a href=\"https://www.ahrq.gov/teamstepps-program/curriculum/team/tools/index.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">AHRQ team leadership tools ↗</a>. No affiliation, endorsement, or independently validated score is implied.</div><div class=\"adv-source\">Original Carecraft educational activity. Inspired by the structure of <a href=\"https://www.ihi.org/library/tools/quality-improvement-essentials-toolkit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">IHI quality-improvement tools ↗</a>. No affiliation, endorsement, or independently validated score is implied.</div><p>Additional format inspiration: <a href=\"https://5mgame.lxp.academy.who.int/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">WHO My 5 Moments game</a> and <a href=\"https://www.resus.org.uk/public-resource/how-we-save-lives/lifesaver-learning\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">RCUK Lifesaver</a>. These references are examples, not a popularity ranking.</p><h3>Carecraft / Healthcare Crown Academy edition</h3><p>The HCA visual identity uses the supplied crest family and self-hosted Playfair Display and Inter. Photographs retain the original app’s image credits. The care-room explorer is original CSS 3D geometry, not a clinical procedure animation. New question variants and scenario finales are original educational practice and have not undergone independent clinical peer review.</p><p>No certificate, registration security number, payment details or private brand-kit document is embedded in this app.</p>"};
/* Topic-specific teaching notes extend the canonical three-step reading. */
const LESSON_NOTES={
 communication:['Notice whether the person has understood the purpose of the conversation, not just heard the words. A calm opening creates room for their concern before you explain yours.','When a response sounds reassuring but vague, ask what the person expects to happen next. Summarise the agreed action in ordinary language and invite a correction.'],
 safety:['A reliable check links the right person, current record and intended action. Treat an unexplained mismatch as information to resolve before moving on.','At a transition, name who will clarify a discrepancy and where the corrected plan will be recorded. A near miss is useful learning only when the system can see it.'],
 empathy:['Empathy begins with a specific observation or question, then leaves space for the answer. It does not require guessing how someone feels.','Acknowledge the impact you hear, explain what you can do within your role, and make the next contact or action clear. Notice when an assumption has replaced curiosity.'],
 sbar:['Prepare the one decision or response you need before choosing a format. Situation and background should orient the listener; assessment and recommendation make the next step actionable.','Close the loop by confirming who will act, what they understood and when you will reconnect. If the concern remains unresolved, use the local escalation route.'],
 infection:['Think of prevention as a sequence around a task rather than a single ritual. The right action depends on the point of care, exposure risk and local guidance.','Watch for the moment an apparently clean workflow changes: touching a shared surface, moving between tasks or removing protection. Pause and reset the sequence before continuing.'],
 reasoning:['Separate an observation from your interpretation of it. Write down what is known, what is uncertain and what finding would change your working view.','A second plausible explanation is a prompt to seek information, not a reason to delay needed escalation. Revisit the working view when new evidence arrives.'],
 speakup:['A concern is easier to act on when it names an observation, its possible consequence and a request. Use plain language without implying certainty you do not have.','If the first attempt is not heard, repeat the concern more directly and follow the escalation pathway. Afterwards, reflect on whether the team made speaking up easier or harder.'],
 shared:['Begin with what matters to the person and which options are actually available. Explain the tradeoffs in a way they can use, including uncertainty.','A preference expressed after an explanation may differ from the first answer. Check understanding and document the decision and any follow-up question.'],
 ai:['Treat generated output as an unverified draft. Ask what data it used, what might be missing and whether the proposed action is supported by an accountable source.','Protect confidential information under local rules and verify important claims independently. Responsibility for a clinical decision remains with the authorised human team.'],
 wellbeing:['A brief pause can make a demand easier to notice, but it does not fix an unsafe workload. Distinguish what you can change now from what needs team or organisational action.','Name a practical boundary, request help early and use available support. Reflect without turning normal strain into a personal failure.'],
 equity:['Ask what barriers are present in this encounter rather than assuming they follow from identity. Access, language, time and trust can all alter what a reasonable plan looks like.','Offer a way to correct your understanding and check whether the proposed next step is feasible. Escalate structural barriers through the appropriate service route.'],
 improvement:['Define a problem as an observable process before choosing a solution. A small test should specify what changed, where, and how you will know whether it helped.','Look for unintended effects as well as the desired outcome. Share what failed, adjust the idea and measure again before declaring a new standard.'],
 'health-literacy':['The burden of making an explanation understandable belongs with the service, not with the learner or patient. Replace jargon and divide a plan into manageable actions.','Ask the person to describe the plan in their own words and repair any gap without blame. An interpreter or accessible format may be needed for a fair conversation.'],
 'risk-evidence':['A percentage has no meaning without its denominator, time period and population. Ask whether the comparison is absolute or relative and whether the groups resemble the person in front of you.','Describe benefits and harms on the same scale when possible. If the evidence does not apply cleanly, state that uncertainty rather than presenting a false precision.'],
 'human-factors':['Design a workflow for real interruptions, limited attention and handoffs. A reminder helps only if the right person sees it at the right decision point.','When an error occurs, examine conditions and signals before attributing it to carelessness. Test a system change with the people who do the work.'],
 'med-transitions':['A list copied forward can appear complete while missing a recent change. Compare the best available history with the intended plan and identify who can confirm a discrepancy.','Make additions, changes and stops visible to the next team and to the person taking the medicines. Follow local reconciliation and prescribing responsibilities.'],
 deterioration:['Notice a meaningful change from the person’s baseline as well as a single measurement. Combine the observation, trend and concern in an escalation that requests a response.','Reassess while help is being arranged according to local protocol. Confirm who has accepted responsibility and document the handover and response.'],
 'data-stewardship':['Before collecting or sharing information, ask whether it is needed for the task and whether the recipient is authorised. Convenience is not a reason to widen access.','Use approved systems, check the recipient and report a suspected breach promptly through local channels. Explain data use honestly when the person asks.'],
 'teachback-skill':['Teach-back checks the clarity of your explanation. Frame it as your responsibility: ask the person to show how they will use the plan, rather than testing them.','If their account differs, change your wording or medium and try again. Confirm the action and where they can seek help if circumstances change.'],
 conflict:['Identify the shared purpose before debating a proposed action. Describe the disputed observation and its impact without assigning motives.','Invite the other perspective, then agree on a safe next step and ownership. If the disagreement creates an immediate risk, use the appropriate escalation route.'],
 documentation:['Write for the next person who must understand what happened and what to do. Distinguish observed facts, decisions, outstanding questions and named follow-up.','Check that timing, authorship and amendments are clear under local policy. Avoid vague reassurance when a concern still needs review.'],
 'risk-dialogue':['Start with the decision the person faces and the outcomes they care about. Present comparable options and make uncertainty explicit.','Check whether the numbers are understood and whether the choice is workable in their context. Record the agreed plan and a route to revisit it.'],
 feedback:['Useful feedback describes an observable action and its effect, then invites the recipient’s view. Keep the focus on a change that can be practised.','Agree on the next attempt and when to review it. A broad label about someone’s character is much harder to act on than a specific example.'],
 debrief:['Create a short, respectful account of what happened before deciding what should change. Let people describe the conditions they saw, including missing information.','Separate immediate support from later system learning. Record an owner and a review date for any proposed improvement so the discussion leads somewhere.'],
 humility:['Confidence can help action, but certainty should remain open to new facts. State what would change your mind and invite a view that differs from yours.','When corrected, update the plan visibly and credit the contribution. This makes it safer for others to question a decision next time.'],
 dignity:['Privacy, language and choice shape the experience of a routine task. Ask permission, explain what will happen and notice who else can hear or see.','If urgency limits choice, explain the reason and restore as much control as possible. Small acts of respect are part of the workflow, not a separate extra.'],
 'just-culture':['Describe what the system made easy or difficult before judging an individual action. Distinguish an honest mistake from a deliberate choice using a fair local process.','Support those affected and create a route for learning that does not hide the event. A proposed fix should address the conditions that contributed to it.'],
 accountability:['Owning an action means naming its scope, the person responsible and the time for review. Make dependencies visible when the next step belongs to another role.','If a commitment cannot be met, communicate early and agree on a revised plan. Follow-through is observable; a promise alone is not.'],
 'compassion-boundaries':['Compassionate care includes a clear, sustainable role. Acknowledge a need, say what you can offer and arrange an appropriate handoff when more support is required.','Notice when repeated extra work hides a service gap. Ask the team to address that gap rather than treating unlimited availability as the standard.'],
 'inclusive-leadership':['Whose perspective is absent from this decision? Invite specific observations from quieter or less senior colleagues before announcing a preferred answer.','Respond constructively when a concern changes the plan. Make ownership and feedback visible so contributors know their input mattered.']
};

